WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.040
KASTNER: Experiments
were done up the street.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.870
And if you walk across through
Building 9 into Building 13,

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.950
you can find the offices
where Bob Laughlin yelled

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.440
at me and his thesis
advisor, John Joannopoulos,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.220
for many years.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.920
We really feel as though
this is a family affair.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.730
And we're really delighted
that Bob and Dan and Horst

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:40.350
all agreed to come
and speak today.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.130
I want to express
thanks on behalf

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:48.450
of the Physics Department
to Dave Litster,

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.730
the Vice President for
research and Dean for research

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.080
for helping to support this.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.220
And for the Francis
Bitter Magnet Lab.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.560
Bob Griffin also
helped support it.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:06.210
And I especially want to mention
the David Harris lecture fund.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.010
David Harris was
a student at MIT.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:13.520
And before his death, he
discussed with his family

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.210
a memorial that he would like.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.370
And he wanted a
memorial which would

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.880
be a living memorial as
opposed to hardware or bricks

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:21.930
and mortar.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:27.360
And so his family endowed a fund
which provides resources for us

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.390
to bring distinguished lectures
to the physics department

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.100
each year.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.040
And this year we're especially
delighted that that's

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.050
being used to help
support this symposium.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.450
So now, I'd like to call
upon Dave Litster, who

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.350
would like to say a few words.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:47.850
Dave.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.050
LITSTER: Thank you, Marc.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.400
It says here another welcome.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.080
I'm not sure you
need another welcome.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.530
But I think the
reason I was asked

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:13.900
to do this is to indicate
that this lecture series is

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.330
an important part of
the intellectual life

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:19.690
of the Institute as a whole
and that what's happening

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:23.710
this afternoon is also important
to the rest of the Institute

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:27.040
and not just the
Physics Department.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.010
So I welcome you.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.790
I was thinking about
this prize a little bit.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.850
And the thing that-- the
idea that popped into my head

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:42.640
is that, very often, those
of us who do basic science,

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.550
experimental basic
science, especially

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:49.660
owe an enormous debt of
gratitude to technology.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.630
But we seldom
acknowledge it publicly.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.190
But it's very important.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.100
And this Nobel Prize
would never have

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:02.830
happened without some very
important pieces of technology.

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.760
Sometimes, the basic scientists
have to develop the technology

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:07.780
themselves.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.140
And here, I see there were
three important technological

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.650
ingredients.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:16.360
The first was these
very special materials

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.030
with very high
electron mobility.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:23.110
And those were developed
by Horst and Dan

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.810
together with Art Gossard
at Bell Laboratories.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:30.510
Without those, the experiments
could not have been done.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.840
Secondly, the
experiments required

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.580
very high magnetic fields.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.640
And those happened to be
available at the Francis Bitter

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:40.890
Laboratory and no place
else in the country.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.830
And so the experiments
were done here.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.170
I think Peter Wolff
will be telling us

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:48.827
more later this evening for
those who were there about it.

00:03:48.827 --> 00:03:50.660
And the third bit of
technology is something

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:54.930
that people refer to
as wet technology.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.820
It was the unusual brain
of Bob Laughlin who

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.560
happens to be able
to think about things

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.420
in a way different
from most of us

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:05.750
and, therefore, was
able to explain this.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:08.580
So I think it's going to be
an interesting afternoon.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.140
In fact, it's going to be
a real intellectual treat.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:15.970
And I will sit down so that
Bob Griffin can proceed

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:17.240
and we can get it started.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:17.739
Thank you.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.880
GRIFFIN: Well, thank
you, Marc and Dave.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.550
I'm here because I'm
Director, presently,

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.670
the Director of the Francis
Bitter Magnet Laboratory.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:50.360
And as many of you know, being
Director of the Magnet Lab

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:53.270
has been like being
captain of a--

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.570
the last few years, at least--
being captain of a ship sailing

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.570
on a really pretty rough sea.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.160
Some people-- one
of my colleagues

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:06.950
described us like being
captain of the Titanic even.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:12.530
But we've had some very good
experiences, nevertheless

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:13.730
during the last few years.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:17.180
And many of those were
associated with visits

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.340
from Horst Stormer and Dan Tsui.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:21.410
I mean, we're very closely
connected, of course,

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.620
with the experimental aspects
of the fractional quantum Hall

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:26.600
effect.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.470
As Dave Litster just mentioned,
the fractional quantum Hall

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:33.410
effect was actually discovered
at the Francis Bitter National

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.660
Magnet Laboratory at that time.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:38.390
And it was actually
approximately 18 years ago

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:40.700
today, yesterday rather.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:45.440
I don't know if you realize
that, October 7, 1981.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.450
And we're converging
to the opinion

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:49.070
that the experiment
may have been done

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:51.260
in cell four of the Magnet Lab.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:55.460
Maybe Horst and Dan can
comment on that later on.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:06:01.220
But we owe Horst and Dan a very
great thank you, a very big

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:01.760
thank you.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.690
They have been an
enormous help to the lab.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.600
And I think
everybody at the lab,

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.030
particularly myself,
really appreciates what

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:09.430
they have done for the lab.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:13.590
And perhaps more than
whatever we did for them.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:17.780
And so I would like to express
the gratitude of the laboratory

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:20.600
and its members to both of
those individuals for all

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.000
of the nice
interactions we've had.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.320
And also to add our
congratulations to everyone

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:40.180
here on the award of
the 1998 Nobel Prize.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:41.860
KASTNER: Thanks, Bob.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.230
So now I'm going to
call on Ray Ashoori, who

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:47.150
was post-doc with Horst
Stormer at Bell Labs

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:50.920
before he came to the MIT
faculty to introduce Horst.

00:06:59.807 --> 00:07:01.890
ASHOORI: Well, it's a
pleasure and an honor for me

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:03.410
to introduce Horst.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:05.820
Horst has been at Bell
Labs since completing

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:10.530
his PhD at the University
of Stuttgart in 1977.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.030
And he started at
Bell as a post-doc

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.740
and a year later
became a regular member

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.230
of technical staff.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:18.450
And following sort of a meteoric
rise, three or four years

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.230
later became a
department head, head

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:23.130
of the famous semiconductor
physics department

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:26.700
which is where the
transistor was invented.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.340
And he became, after that,
director of physics research

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:30.720
in 1991.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:32.940
He's now a professor
at Columbia.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:37.170
And he still holds an adjunct
director position at Bell.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.470
By the mid-1980s, when
I was in grad school,

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:41.820
Horst was already quite famous.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:45.330
In fact, I remember one
of the older students

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:50.440
in my lab having boasted,
I saw Horst Stormer.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:54.780
And a lot of other adjectives
came along with that.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.810
But you can imagine how
thrilled I was when he chose me

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:03.440
as a post-doc back in 1990.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:07.600
So the first thing I
noticed coming into the lab

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.960
was just an amazing
amount of inventiveness

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.940
and a little bit
of outrageousness

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:16.250
that I think led Horst to
do a lot of great things.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:18.730
The first or second day
in the lab, he asked me,

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:19.440
can you solder?

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.370
I said, sure, I can solder.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.380
I thought it was just
soldering wires together.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.920
What he meant was,
can you sit up

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:31.490
on this table on top of a stool
and solder a couple of wires--

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:36.490
solder, under a microscope, on
the end of a long probe that we

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:39.610
used, a low
temperature probe, and

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:45.130
solder little microscopic wires
on this sort of flimsy set up.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:46.970
And the idea was--
we had an idea--

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:49.510
he had an idea for a
very novel experiment.

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:53.650
And I was a little bit reticent
about sitting up on the stool.

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:57.220
So he showed me first that
it could, indeed, be done.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:02.440
So it's this kind of
spirit that made it

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:07.900
just a tremendous amount
of fun to be there.

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:10.390
Way back when he
was a post-doc, he

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:12.400
invented a technique
for growing ultra

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:16.210
pure semiconductor materials
called modulation doping.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:19.330
And this discovery--
or this invention

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:22.210
was one of the key
items, I think,

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:26.200
in finally giving rise to the
discovery of fractional quantum

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:30.260
Hall effect that he and Dan Tsui
did here, as you've all heard.

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:36.520
And this kind of
structure, actually-- it

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:38.860
has had some
technological relevance,

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.780
in that, if you
have a cell phone,

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.820
you've got transistors
made out of this kind

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:46.410
of ultra pure material
that Horst invented.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:49.390
And some of these ideas that
he's had, in retrospect they

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:52.750
seem so simple and
maybe even obvious.

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:55.210
But of course, before
you make the discovery,

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:58.390
before you have the idea,
it's not at all obvious.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:02.410
And Horst is known,
I think, for just

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:07.790
seeing with amazing clarity
what needs to be done.

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:10.570
Here's another sort of
outrageous thing that he did.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:14.060
He invented a technique called
cleaved edged overgrowth, which

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:15.910
you take a wafer that you grow.

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:17.710
It's very thin wafer.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:21.550
You cleave it inside
of a machine that's

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:23.380
used for growing these wafers.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:26.781
And you actually grow on
this thin edge of a wafer.

00:10:26.781 --> 00:10:27.280
OK.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.170
This allows you to
make, it turns out,

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:32.050
perfect one-dimensional
structures.

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:35.950
And the technique itself is
difficult enough, but on top

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:39.250
of this, when I came to his lab
he's actually doing lithography

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:43.040
on this half-millimeter edge.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:46.220
So imagine doing lithography--
the kind that's normally done

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:47.960
on big semiconductor wafers--

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:50.150
on a very tiny edge.

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:54.980
And I was just amazed at the
outrageousness of this quest.

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:56.440
He actually made it work.

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.150
And he has done beautiful
1D experiments--

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:01.760
experiments on
one-dimensional wires since.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:04.690
I also-- here, I'm blessed
with being near Peter Wolff who

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:06.980
was Director of the
Magnet Lab at the time

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.230
Horst and Dan made
the discovery.

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:13.700
And I just love listening to
his stories about Horst and Dan

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:16.490
during that time and how they--

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:18.920
when they discovered the
fractional quantum Hall effect.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:20.420
First of all, they're
working inside

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:25.010
of a very noisy big magnet where
there's giant currents running

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:27.050
through the spinner
magnet, water

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:29.750
rushing through it, making
the whole system vibrate.

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:33.050
This bore is about
two centimeters wide.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:36.740
Now, imagine you have room
temperature outside this bore.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:39.950
Inside the bore, you want
it to be 50 millikelvin.

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:42.860
So you somehow have to
develop a refrigerator that

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:45.580
cools your samples
down to 50 millikelvin.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:48.050
Horst and Dan did it,
and that, of course,

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:49.630
is what got them the prize.

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:53.240
So you can see how this
kind of inventiveness

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:57.124
is just critical to
this kind of discovery.

00:11:57.124 --> 00:11:58.790
Here's another story
about the first day

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:00.230
on the job with Horst.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:01.880
He has tremendous energy.

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:04.250
And you see this in
the way he walks,

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:06.560
because you just can't
keep up with him.

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:12.470
So what I would notice is that
he walks so fast that I would--

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:13.590
I couldn't walk as fast.

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:15.470
So I'd end up
running a few steps,

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:17.790
then walking a few steps,
and running a few steps.

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:21.080
And this is how we
progressed to lunch every day

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:22.800
during my two
years of Bell Labs.

00:12:25.109 --> 00:12:26.650
After Horst became
director, and that

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:32.530
was after my first year
at Bell, after a year,

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.300
he would still come
down to the lab,

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.580
and he would do things like
help me change the pump oil.

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:41.622
You know, what a completely
mundane job for Nobel

00:12:41.622 --> 00:12:42.580
Prize-winning director.

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:44.840
He's getting up because
he's getting sick of this.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:47.890
But you could see
you could see in him

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:52.300
that there was a real love
of just being in the lab.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:53.550
All right.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:54.830
Let me end with this.

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:57.840
Horst has received every physics
award imaginable, the Buckley

00:12:57.840 --> 00:12:59.970
Prize, the Nobel Prize,
the Franklin Medal,

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:01.870
he's in the National
Academy of Sciences.

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:04.280
He's a truly, I think,
extraordinary and rare person.

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:06.930
And it's just a thrill to have
them here just for the day.

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:08.318
Here's Horst.

00:13:08.318 --> 00:13:17.030
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:18.160
STORMER: Is this on?

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:19.290
Is this on?

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:20.140
SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:22.015
STORMER: Ray, you haven't
gotten any briefer.

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:28.320
But thanks very much.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:32.601
I'm-- it's just too much honor.

00:13:32.601 --> 00:13:33.100
Thanks.

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:36.395
Thanks very-- I must say--

00:13:36.395 --> 00:13:38.020
you hear more and
more of these things,

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:39.910
and you start wondering
about yourself.

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:42.760
But, all I can say, I'm
a very lucky guy that

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:44.970
has worked with
tremendous people,

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.050
and not just collaborators
but also then

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:50.230
in the form of post-docs,
and Ray is one of those.

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:51.730
And there are several
others around.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:52.850
And they give me
all this credit.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:53.835
And it's just not true.

00:13:53.835 --> 00:13:55.630
It's these guys that did it.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:58.442
And that Ray says help changing
pump oil was very easy.

00:13:58.442 --> 00:13:59.650
It's the only thing I can do.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:02.160
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:04.600
It's a pleasure
to be back at what

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:07.990
was, for many years,
our part-time home,

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:09.040
not far down here.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:11.560
Sort of a place
you really wouldn't

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:13.450
want to call home
when you walk into it.

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:15.940
It's a loud and
noisy environment.

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:20.140
But Dan Tsui and I came
there for many years

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:24.730
doing our experiments,
eating cold pizza

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:29.800
and drinking warm Coke, looking
at data on chart recorder

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:34.030
coming out, and singing
together with our grad students

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:37.350
funny songs from Monty Python.

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:41.120
It was a really good
time for a few years

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:43.120
that-- well, actually,
it must be about a decade

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:44.830
that we came up here.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:46.525
So it's a pleasure
to be back at MIT.

00:14:46.525 --> 00:14:49.780
It doesn't mean that I haven't
been back in the meantime

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:52.760
visiting old friends, and many
of them are in the audience.

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:54.886
There's actually too many
to go through one by one.

00:14:54.886 --> 00:14:57.010
If I do it, I know I'm
going to forget one of them,

00:14:57.010 --> 00:15:00.130
so I better don't.

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:01.330
So we have three hours.

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:03.250
I'm not sure the
audience how long

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:05.480
you're going to hold
out for three hours.

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:08.420
But I have the advantage
of having the first hour,

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:10.005
and Bob has the last hour.

00:15:10.005 --> 00:15:12.507
[LAUGHS] So let's see
what we're going to do.

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:21.620
That's my first--
can I take this off?

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:22.780
No.

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:24.790
Almost not.

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:28.720
You can imagine we're doing
a lot of these recently.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:31.660
Actually, the two of us, Bob
and I, have been on a tour

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:33.490
through Scandinavia.

00:15:33.490 --> 00:15:38.840
And we started feeling very
much like these gentlemen.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:40.772
It's just a good one that--

00:15:40.772 --> 00:15:44.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:47.940
And it hasn't it hasn't
stopped much since then.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:51.840
I'm looking very much forward
to Wednesday, next week.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:53.880
I hope that some
of the burden is

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:55.800
going to be taken off from us.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:58.732
But I'm afraid it's not going
to be condensed metaphysics,

00:15:58.732 --> 00:16:00.990
so it still will be rough going.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:03.180
In fact, when I told Bob that--

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:05.350
I did this when we
went to Stockholm,

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:07.080
and I had only the
pieces with me.

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.730
I told Bob that I
would be doing this.

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:12.398
And he said, yeah, and I knew
it who's going to be Pavarotti.

00:16:12.398 --> 00:16:17.550
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:21.480
I told him, of course, the best.

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:24.340
So I'm actually--
along in this vein,

00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:28.140
I thought, this is, of course,
all this it's a great honor

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:30.000
and it is a wonderful,
wonderful week,

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.870
and we had a very good time.

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:37.080
And how good a time we had
is perhaps shown in this one.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:41.850
You come in in the morning, and
you are sleepy and not shaved

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:44.610
and washed, because
you get off the plane.

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:46.590
And so they want you
to take a photograph

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:48.810
in front of the King.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.700
This is the King.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:52.802
Yeah.

00:16:52.802 --> 00:16:56.610
I remember, I swear,
Lou told a Viagra joke.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:58.240
No.

00:16:58.240 --> 00:16:59.790
I tell you why we're laughing.

00:16:59.790 --> 00:17:01.950
And you know why we're laughing.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:05.760
The Viagra jokes
were old by then.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:07.650
So they get you in
front of the camera.

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:10.657
And what all photographers
do is-- you're never

00:17:10.657 --> 00:17:11.490
close enough, right?

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:12.459
Photographers always do that.

00:17:12.459 --> 00:17:14.292
And because they have
one hand on the camera

00:17:14.292 --> 00:17:15.480
they do it like this.

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:17.619
So they wanted us to
stand closer together.

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:21.730
And, you know, Bob
and I say, I can't.

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:23.349
He's, after all, in
the middle there,

00:17:23.349 --> 00:17:26.349
and it's not so easy to get--

00:17:26.349 --> 00:17:28.000
so Bob started out complaining.

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:29.456
He said, what do you want?

00:17:29.456 --> 00:17:31.210
And I started laughing.

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:32.950
So the two of us
just blurted out.

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:35.950
And you see this is
Louis Ignarro, one

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:37.877
of the physiology prize winners.

00:17:37.877 --> 00:17:38.710
And he looks around.

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:41.050
He's just totally
flabbergasted at how poorly--

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:45.054
how badly physicists can
behave in front of the kings.

00:17:45.054 --> 00:17:46.720
Well, of course, I
mean, it was that one

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:50.440
that ended up in the
newspaper the next day.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:54.640
My fellow countrymen have
had good fun with me.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:57.085
Because, in fact,
there was always this--

00:17:57.085 --> 00:18:00.820
it raises sort of the question,
is he German, or is he not?

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:05.050
Can we can we count him
as one of ours or not?

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:08.350
And this question actually
goes on, and particularly

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:09.460
because my umlaut--

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:11.620
I have an umlaut in my
name, and I dropped it

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:13.420
when I came to this country.

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:17.860
Because a good friend of
mine, Michael Schluter,

00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:20.260
who is no more--

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:22.330
but he told me drop you umlaut.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:26.280
Because A, the typewriter
doesn't have it.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:27.830
They don't know
what to do with it.

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:31.640
And then the smart guys
put an E afterwards.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:33.320
So half of them drop the umlaut.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:35.939
The other half puts
an E after the O.

00:18:35.939 --> 00:18:37.730
Then you have appear
twice in the alphabet,

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.250
and it's not next to each other.

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:42.710
So I dropped my umlaut.

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:45.217
But, now, of course, they
have found the umlaut again.

00:18:45.217 --> 00:18:47.300
And you wouldn't believe,
in the last APS meeting,

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:51.572
I was in twice, once with
and once without umlaut.

00:18:51.572 --> 00:18:53.780
But this is what, actually,
somebody from [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:57.390
sent me the next day in
the mail from Germany.

00:18:57.390 --> 00:18:59.510
This was-- this is sort
of the equivalent of the,

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:01.850
I don't know, daily
news probably.

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:03.330
This was the front page of it.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:08.120
And when I got this in the
mail, my first reaction

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:12.020
is to fly home and
kill my mother.

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:12.890
Because that's me--

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:15.265
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:15.265 --> 00:19:21.110
--as a five-year-old
playing Indians and Cowboys.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:25.130
In Germany, then, of course,
no concept of the whole thing.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:27.520
But I thought my mother
had given him that picture.

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:29.270
But indeed, they had
shot it off the wall,

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:31.686
because it's hanging together
with my little brother who's

00:19:31.686 --> 00:19:32.950
here that they cut out.

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:36.260
It's hanging on the wall in my
life my parents' living room.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:39.980
In Germany-- this is, here,
referring to the picture

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:43.100
our Nobel laureate.

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:46.040
And then it says under
here, already as a boy,

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:48.900
he built a toy train with
a rocket propulsion, which

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:52.230
is actually-- which is true.

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:53.520
What it doesn't say there.

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:55.580
So in very small letters here--

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.500
and, by the way, it
blew up in his hand

00:19:57.500 --> 00:19:59.932
and ripped off his thumb,
which is also true.

00:19:59.932 --> 00:20:00.890
It was a long time ago.

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:02.390
I have no problem
with this anymore.

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:06.290
But it certainly doesn't show me
as a very good experimentalist.

00:20:06.290 --> 00:20:09.530
So it was a little bit off.

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:12.950
Well, enough of the jokes.

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:16.897
Let me try to talk a bit about
the fractional quantum Hall

00:20:16.897 --> 00:20:18.230
effect and see how far I get in.

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:23.760
And Dan said that however I
get, he takes the second half.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:28.100
So I want to talk about
fractional charges

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:30.540
and fractional quantum numbers.

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:33.170
And I want to give you
sort of an impression what

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:34.246
this whole thing about.

00:20:34.246 --> 00:20:36.620
The way I judge the audience
is actually that most of you

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:38.036
actually know
everything about it,

00:20:38.036 --> 00:20:40.360
and I might as well sit down.

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:42.930
But, after all, you
paid my ticket up here.

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:44.800
So why don't I deliver.

00:20:48.519 --> 00:20:50.560
I don't think I have to
say much to this audience

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:53.930
about condensed metaphysics.

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:56.170
But I think-- let
me just, perhaps,

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:58.120
pick up at one point
along this thought.

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:01.360
And that is one of
the big problems

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:02.980
nowadays in condense
metaphysics is

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:06.220
electron-electron correlations,
meaning that electrons

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:08.440
are strongly interacting,
of course, because they

00:21:08.440 --> 00:21:10.030
are charged.

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:12.760
And taking into account
the correlation,

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:15.430
the motion between
electrons as it's

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:18.540
forced upon them by the
other electrons around them

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:20.440
is a very difficult problem.

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:22.390
Most of through our
life, we get away

00:21:22.390 --> 00:21:25.150
with very crude approximations.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:26.830
Number one, forget it.

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:28.992
Just don't take it into account.

00:21:28.992 --> 00:21:30.700
Twiddle a little bit
around with the math

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:34.400
and with that dispersion
relation, and you're done.

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:36.700
But, in some cases,
electron-electron interaction

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:38.014
is everything.

00:21:38.014 --> 00:21:39.430
For example, in
superconductivity,

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:43.130
it's everything, mediated
by phonons there.

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:45.550
And what I want to
show is another case

00:21:45.550 --> 00:21:48.540
where electron-electron
interaction is everything.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:52.240
And it's only electrons this
time around, electrons in 2D

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:54.880
that really show very
strange behavior, indeed.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:57.280
In fact, the strange
behavior that it shows--

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.620
and it's just the pool of
two-dimension electrons,

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:01.660
nothing else, in
a magnetic field.

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:03.220
You get fractional
charges out, which

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:05.720
was, of course, a big surprise.

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:08.260
The new electron quantum
liquids, fractional quantum

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:09.790
numbers, flux--

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:12.890
magnetic flux can be
attached to electrons.

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:15.220
At least, it looks as if it
were attached to electrons.

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:17.290
New particles are popping
up that can sometimes

00:22:17.290 --> 00:22:19.600
be fermions and bosons,
depending on what

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:21.250
magnetic fields you have.

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:24.190
Huge magnetic fields, as big
as the ones that you get next

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:26.980
door-- or got next door, and
you still get a good fraction

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:28.750
of them next door--

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:32.090
can apparently disappear, 10
Tesla, or something like that,

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:34.870
a million times the Earth's
magnetic field, disappears.

00:22:34.870 --> 00:22:36.460
And the carriers
that you have seem

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:39.810
to be moving along as if
there were no magnetic field.

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:41.720
There's a mass
popping up, which has

00:22:41.720 --> 00:22:44.250
nothing to do with the
mass of the electron.

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:47.150
And they may even
superconduct in some sense.

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:49.040
Let me see whether
I get to this,

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:50.977
give you a bit of an impression.

00:22:50.977 --> 00:22:53.060
We're working on two-dimensional
electron systems.

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:54.590
And you can create
two-dimensional electron

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:56.548
system, and three-dimensional
worlds are always

00:22:56.548 --> 00:22:59.496
at an interface
between-- can you see--

00:22:59.496 --> 00:23:02.177
always at an interface
between two things.

00:23:02.177 --> 00:23:04.010
So you could do it on
the surface of helium,

00:23:04.010 --> 00:23:05.926
and it is being done on
the surface of helium,

00:23:05.926 --> 00:23:07.610
on the surface of anything.

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:09.210
Typically, things are dirty.

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:10.710
This is no comment
about your floor.

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:13.940
It's just dirty, in the
sense microscopically,

00:23:13.940 --> 00:23:17.145
that there are dangling bonds
where the electrons get stuck.

00:23:17.145 --> 00:23:18.770
So it turns out,
actually, the best way

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:20.186
to make a
two-dimensional electron

00:23:20.186 --> 00:23:22.880
system is that the interface
between two things,

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:27.710
between two semiconductors
inside a semiconductor.

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:29.810
The technique to do
this was actually

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:36.350
developed, invented, in the
'70s by Al Cho at Bell Labs.

00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:39.200
And it's a high vacuum
evaporation technique.

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:41.060
We basically evaporate
two semiconductors

00:23:41.060 --> 00:23:43.120
on top of each other.

00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:45.320
And so this is a
high vacuum chamber.

00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:47.430
And you evaporate
gallium and arsenic,

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:48.630
gives you one semiconductor.

00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:50.340
Gallium and aluminum
and arsenic gives you

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:51.256
another semiconductor.

00:23:51.256 --> 00:23:53.086
That's all you need to know.

00:23:53.086 --> 00:23:54.460
And you grow it
on the substrate,

00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:56.695
which provides a template
so that the atoms go

00:23:56.695 --> 00:23:57.570
into the right place.

00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.820
Otherwise, it's just like
ball bearings, in the sense,

00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:01.270
otherwise, it would be random.

00:24:01.270 --> 00:24:03.840
So you provided a nice
underlying lattice for it

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:05.160
to grow.

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:07.561
And, as you know, in order
for semiconductors to conduct,

00:24:07.561 --> 00:24:09.060
you have to throw
some dirt into it.

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:10.530
You have to doping them.

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:13.330
And, in this case, actually
silicon becomes the doping.

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:15.030
So silicon is the
stuff that gives off

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:17.670
an additional electron
that then can move around

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:20.350
in the material.

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:23.160
The way this looks is like this.

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:25.710
This is about two-inch diameter.

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:26.957
What is dark here--

00:24:26.957 --> 00:24:28.290
disregard these things up there.

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:29.831
They actually do
have something to do

00:24:29.831 --> 00:24:31.620
with this what Ray brought up.

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:33.370
And that is this cleave
that you overgrow.

00:24:33.370 --> 00:24:34.540
This is not import.

00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:35.790
It's just this part down here.

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:37.090
This is the wafer.

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:39.990
So they're shiny semiconductors,
look like a silicon wafer,

00:24:39.990 --> 00:24:41.790
basically.

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:45.030
And the reason that the black
here is because the background

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:45.530
was black.

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:48.085
But this is a shiny surface of
about 2-inch diameter, which

00:24:48.085 --> 00:24:51.730
then has been grown by MBE.

00:24:51.730 --> 00:24:56.350
And the important aspect of
this is that you have incredibly

00:24:56.350 --> 00:24:58.500
controlled in this E direction.

00:24:58.500 --> 00:25:03.842
You can grow these layers
one layer after the other.

00:25:03.842 --> 00:25:05.550
So what you see here
in the cross-section

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:07.260
is four layers of
gallium arsenide,

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:09.330
four layers of
aluminum arsenide,

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:10.730
four layers of gallium arsenide.

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:13.299
This is the black
and white stripes.

00:25:13.299 --> 00:25:14.090
You have a control.

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:16.950
This was in one atomic layer.

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:21.030
And this is-- so you
form these layers that

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:23.370
fluctuate by not more
than one atomic layer, so

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:25.200
about an angstrom.

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:27.540
So this is the flattest
surfaces that you

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:31.670
can create with a control
of, really, one layer.

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:35.500
You know you can stop at
five, or six, or seven layers.

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:39.780
And this technique that
was developed and invented

00:25:39.780 --> 00:25:43.020
in the 1970s we had
then used in order

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:47.450
to make very high mobility
two-dimension electron system.

00:25:47.450 --> 00:25:49.830
In three dimensions,
there's sort of a catch-22

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:50.830
when you dope something.

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:53.180
You throw in the
silicon, which gives off

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:55.700
the electron that then moves
through the semiconductor.

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:58.340
But your left with the charged
the impurities of which

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:00.960
the electron then, very
often, bounces off.

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:02.540
So, yes, you introduce carriers.

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:05.290
But you also introduce
a scattering for it.

00:26:05.290 --> 00:26:08.310
In 3D, you hardly
can get around it.

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:09.560
In 2D, you can.

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:11.360
Because in 2D, you
can put the electrons

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:13.980
in one layer and the
dirt in the other layer.

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:16.160
And therefore, they
hardly see each other.

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:17.930
And this is the method to do it.

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:20.930
It's called modulation
doping, which

00:26:20.930 --> 00:26:22.750
is-- so the green is
one semiconductor.

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:25.310
The blue is the
other semiconductor.

00:26:25.310 --> 00:26:27.520
You put the impurities into
the green semiconductor,

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:29.990
and they wander over into
the blue semiconductor,

00:26:29.990 --> 00:26:32.570
because there they
find lower energy.

00:26:32.570 --> 00:26:36.110
This is a quantum well of
about 100 angstrom thickness.

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:39.075
And the electrons now
can move in this plane.

00:26:39.075 --> 00:26:41.450
But they cannot move in this
E direction because they are

00:26:41.450 --> 00:26:43.001
tightly confined.

00:26:43.001 --> 00:26:45.500
In fact, they are so tightly
confined that quantum mechanics

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:47.420
plays an important role.

00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:49.850
They're confined that you
see the first bound state

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:51.710
and the second bound
state, and they're

00:26:51.710 --> 00:26:55.160
separated by about, let's
say, 10 millielectron volts.

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:58.910
So quantum mechanics is an
important aspect of this.

00:26:58.910 --> 00:27:01.040
If you only have
the lowest state

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:02.570
occupied, and not
the second one,

00:27:02.570 --> 00:27:05.000
the electron in the Z direction
has no degree of freedom.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:06.380
It's just tight.

00:27:06.380 --> 00:27:07.860
It cannot do anything.

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:10.520
But it can move in the
x-y plane easily, in fact,

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:12.650
very readily because
it's such a nice plane,

00:27:12.650 --> 00:27:14.690
and then the impurities
are away from it.

00:27:14.690 --> 00:27:18.050
So you get a very high mobility
two-dimensional electron system

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:21.740
in which the electron can
run for very long distances

00:27:21.740 --> 00:27:24.620
before they scatter as anything.

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:26.780
The electron density
is about 10 to the 11,

00:27:26.780 --> 00:27:28.987
which makes about an
electron every 300 angstrom,

00:27:28.987 --> 00:27:29.820
something like this.

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:30.890
Typically 10 to the 11.

00:27:30.890 --> 00:27:32.750
You can vary, but
that's a typical density

00:27:32.750 --> 00:27:34.280
that you will find.

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:38.180
Mobility is nowadays
as high as 10 million.

00:27:38.180 --> 00:27:39.456
In fact, last week--

00:27:39.456 --> 00:27:40.580
no, it was the week before.

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:44.940
Two weeks ago, we had a
record of 23 million mobility,

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:46.560
which is unheard of.

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:50.250
I mean, I'll show you a
little bit more about this.

00:27:50.250 --> 00:27:52.070
You may not understand
what mobility means.

00:27:52.070 --> 00:27:55.550
But it's a mean-free pass
of about 100 microns.

00:27:55.550 --> 00:27:57.950
In fact, 20 million
is about 200 microns.

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:00.570
So the electron travels in this
plane for about 200 microns

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:03.290
without bouncing into anything.

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:05.270
And 200 microns is really big.

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:07.737
It's about the thickness
of your fingernail, which

00:28:07.737 --> 00:28:08.570
doesn't look so big.

00:28:08.570 --> 00:28:12.050
But on a microscopic scale,
and on a little graphic scale

00:28:12.050 --> 00:28:16.980
what we can do nowadays, it
is a huge mean-free pass.

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:19.200
And it's these two-dimensional
electron system,

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:21.860
which then we can regard
as a two-dimensional metal.

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:25.010
They're basically nothing
else but that with a very long

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:25.760
mean-free pass.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:27.470
So it means that the
lattice behind it

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:28.822
hardly plays any role.

00:28:28.822 --> 00:28:30.530
It's like a two-dimension
electron system

00:28:30.530 --> 00:28:32.920
just in free space.

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:40.040
Now, this technique was
invented around 1978, something

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:41.660
like this, by these four guys.

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:43.590
And I show it to
you for two reasons.

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:45.710
The one is sort of
a historical picture

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:47.060
in terms of MBE machines.

00:28:47.060 --> 00:28:48.900
This is how MBE
machines looked then.

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:52.370
In fact, what you did is
this is a very high vacuum.

00:28:52.370 --> 00:28:54.780
These days, you still open
it up, let all the air in,

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:56.755
stuck your sample in,
pumped it out over night.

00:28:56.755 --> 00:28:58.820
And the next morning,
you get your growth.

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:01.670
You don't do this anymore,
much cleaner than that.

00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:04.930
The people standing around here
is Willy Wiegmann, Art Gossard,

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:08.840
Ray Dingle, and this is me
in an earlier incarnation,

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:11.750
standing around the MBE
machine for the photographer.

00:29:11.750 --> 00:29:13.460
And the important part--

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:16.330
the other important part why I'm
showing to you is Art Gossard.

00:29:16.330 --> 00:29:17.960
Art Gossard was the
person that grew

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:20.320
the sample on which
we then discovered

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:22.580
the fractional
quantum Hall effect.

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:24.200
And this was the
first time mobilities

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:27.260
went as high as 90,000.

00:29:27.260 --> 00:29:29.880
And that made it all possible.

00:29:29.880 --> 00:29:33.560
I mean, it brought
it actually about.

00:29:33.560 --> 00:29:38.120
So the sample is the
most important ingredient

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:39.680
to all of this.

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:41.780
And Art Gossard and his
assistant, Billy Wiegmann,

00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:42.710
they grew the sample.

00:29:42.710 --> 00:29:45.290
And it's really unfortunate
that they didn't

00:29:45.290 --> 00:29:46.640
share with us in that prize.

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:49.040
They shared with us in
some previous prizes,

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:50.960
but not in this one.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:52.670
And I say it's
unfortunate, because I

00:29:52.670 --> 00:29:55.130
think for us condensed
metaphysicists,

00:29:55.130 --> 00:29:59.030
materials is just the most
important aspect of our work.

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:01.790
The people that have
first hand on materials

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:04.370
are the ones that very often
are ahead in the science

00:30:04.370 --> 00:30:07.010
that they're doing because
it's really the materials that

00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:10.210
get them to this initial
step from where you then

00:30:10.210 --> 00:30:11.840
can start playing.

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:18.410
So Art and MBE technology
is a very important aspect

00:30:18.410 --> 00:30:19.940
of all of this.

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:24.320
So what you end up with then is
a two-dimension electron system

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:26.540
where by the separation of
electrons from the doping

00:30:26.540 --> 00:30:29.210
impurities and then the
suppression of scattering from

00:30:29.210 --> 00:30:29.980
those--

00:30:29.980 --> 00:30:31.510
is there any kind of a pointer?

00:30:31.510 --> 00:30:32.010
No?

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:34.640
All right.

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:36.020
You get very high mobilities.

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:38.750
At very low temperatures, they
get a very long mean-free pass.

00:30:38.750 --> 00:30:41.840
So it looks a bit
like a billiard table

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:43.484
in two dimensions.

00:30:43.484 --> 00:30:44.900
Electrons can only
move into two--

00:30:44.900 --> 00:30:45.770
Oh, there is one.

00:30:45.770 --> 00:30:46.269
Right.

00:30:46.269 --> 00:30:50.350
I should-- oh, this is
this little guy there.

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:50.850
Great.

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:54.560
Thanks, Marc.

00:30:54.560 --> 00:30:58.770
Let me show you how an
MBE machine looks nowadays

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:00.910
and the people that
are running it.

00:31:00.910 --> 00:31:06.970
This is the crew nowadays that
is making this 25 million--

00:31:06.970 --> 00:31:08.530
23 million mobility sample.

00:31:08.530 --> 00:31:10.900
Actually, the most important
guy, Loren Pfeiffer,

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:12.310
and his assistant Ken West.

00:31:12.310 --> 00:31:14.980
This is Kirk Baldwin,
who's an assistant with me.

00:31:14.980 --> 00:31:17.990
And this is Amir Yacoby, who
was a post-doc at the time.

00:31:17.990 --> 00:31:20.050
So now, the MBE machine
is about that thick.

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:22.780
And you use much
cleaner methods in order

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:24.070
to get such high mobility.

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:27.040
But again, these are
the important guys

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:29.432
to which we owe everything.

00:31:29.432 --> 00:31:32.640
I'm very often being
asked, why at Bell Labs?

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:34.270
Why at fraction of charges?

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:36.790
Is it ever going to be
in the telephone system?

00:31:36.790 --> 00:31:38.620
And my only answer to
this is this is only

00:31:38.620 --> 00:31:41.900
going to make our vacuum
cleaners run only 1/3 speed,

00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:43.370
and that's about all.

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:45.110
And it's a joke.

00:31:45.110 --> 00:31:49.210
So fractional charges
will not have--

00:31:49.210 --> 00:31:52.540
I don't see fractional charges
having any kind of application,

00:31:52.540 --> 00:31:54.856
even in the long run.

00:31:54.856 --> 00:31:56.230
But there's another
aspect of all

00:31:56.230 --> 00:31:59.350
of this work, which
has an application,

00:31:59.350 --> 00:32:01.170
and that's this one.

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:03.950
Out of exactly the
same material--

00:32:03.950 --> 00:32:07.360
or exactly the same material
in which we discovered,

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:10.020
Dan and I discovered the
fractional quantum Hall

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:11.130
effect--

00:32:11.130 --> 00:32:12.780
out of the same
material, you can

00:32:12.780 --> 00:32:14.684
make field effect transistors.

00:32:14.684 --> 00:32:15.600
It just looks like it.

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:17.550
This is what is in every PC.

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:18.480
It's a MOSFET.

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:19.740
Never mind what it is.

00:32:19.740 --> 00:32:21.480
This thing just looks the same.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:25.070
But it's another material,
and this specific technique

00:32:25.070 --> 00:32:28.050
of modulation doping
creates mobilities

00:32:28.050 --> 00:32:29.940
that are much higher than here.

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:32.760
And contact resistance
are lower in all of this.

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:35.400
So these transistors,
or transistors

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:38.730
made out of this
materials are the fastest

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:42.330
and lowest noise transistors
in the world still today.

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:45.000
This is one built by
Fujitsu, who actually

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:49.240
was one of the first that made
a microscopic transistor out

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:49.740
of this.

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:50.970
Never mind what
the structure is.

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:53.070
But this is the fastest,
lowest noise transistor

00:32:53.070 --> 00:32:54.730
that you can get.

00:32:54.730 --> 00:32:59.770
And it's in any of
those around the world.

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:03.160
Each one has one of
those, radio telescopes.

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:05.390
This is not a very big market.

00:33:05.390 --> 00:33:08.810
There's just about probably
100 or 200 around the world.

00:33:08.810 --> 00:33:11.650
So you cannot make
money with that.

00:33:11.650 --> 00:33:16.850
But you can make quite a lot
of money with these things.

00:33:16.850 --> 00:33:20.410
So a lot of these high frequency
2 gigahertz telephones that--

00:33:20.410 --> 00:33:24.760
cellular phones-- have in the
front end, which is-- well,

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:28.080
the microphone is a
front-end maybe up there--

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:30.360
have one of these
HEM transistors

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:32.260
for very good performance.

00:33:32.260 --> 00:33:34.090
And the base stations
have it in there.

00:33:34.090 --> 00:33:36.700
So there's another
part of this modulation

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:38.640
doping, which is
not basic science,

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:39.640
but, really, technology.

00:33:39.640 --> 00:33:42.160
And I feel very fortunate
that on one side

00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:45.520
this is modulation
doping that we invented

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:48.940
led to very basic science.

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:52.017
And on the other side, it
also led to an application.

00:33:52.017 --> 00:33:54.100
And it's not so that the
one came after the other.

00:33:54.100 --> 00:33:55.580
I think it's an
important lesson.

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:59.080
And we're not the first to
teach this lesson, in a sense.

00:33:59.080 --> 00:34:01.570
Or the material is not the
first to teach this lesson, that

00:34:01.570 --> 00:34:05.740
is that it's not always an
invention, fundamental physics,

00:34:05.740 --> 00:34:07.660
and then there is application.

00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:09.427
Very often, these
things go in parallel,

00:34:09.427 --> 00:34:11.260
and very often, actually,
they go backwards,

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:12.850
because the progress
in technology

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:17.840
is so important for us to do
the next step in science too.

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:18.340
OK.

00:34:18.340 --> 00:34:21.880
Let's get a little bit closer
to the fraction quantum Hall

00:34:21.880 --> 00:34:22.872
effect.

00:34:22.872 --> 00:34:24.830
If you have such a system,
particularly if it's

00:34:24.830 --> 00:34:26.500
semiconductors,
the easiest thing

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:29.020
you can do apart from
measuring its resistance

00:34:29.020 --> 00:34:31.165
is to measure the
Hall resistance.

00:34:31.165 --> 00:34:33.040
All it is it's like a
resistance measurement,

00:34:33.040 --> 00:34:35.299
but in addition, you
put a magnetic field up.

00:34:35.299 --> 00:34:37.090
So this is a two-dimension
electron system.

00:34:37.090 --> 00:34:39.381
You run a current down it,
and you put a magnetic field

00:34:39.381 --> 00:34:40.690
onto it.

00:34:40.690 --> 00:34:42.610
Then you measure two
characteristic voltages,

00:34:42.610 --> 00:34:44.150
the one is along
the current path.

00:34:44.150 --> 00:34:45.979
The other one is across
the current path.

00:34:45.979 --> 00:34:47.770
The one is nothing else
but the resistance,

00:34:47.770 --> 00:34:49.811
and the other one should
be 0, as long as there's

00:34:49.811 --> 00:34:51.879
no magnetic field because
the thing is symmetric.

00:34:51.879 --> 00:34:53.739
But when you put a
magnetic field on it,

00:34:53.739 --> 00:34:56.290
then you push the carriers to
one side because of the Lorentz

00:34:56.290 --> 00:34:57.070
force.

00:34:57.070 --> 00:34:58.366
So you build up a voltage.

00:34:58.366 --> 00:35:00.490
Eventually, you'll build
up a voltage from one side

00:35:00.490 --> 00:35:01.031
to the other.

00:35:01.031 --> 00:35:02.810
That's what you measure.

00:35:02.810 --> 00:35:06.337
And these-- the voltage
that you measure--

00:35:06.337 --> 00:35:08.670
divided by the current, because
it's proportional to it.

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:11.030
So if you double the current,
you get double the voltage.

00:35:11.030 --> 00:35:12.488
So you normalize
it to the current.

00:35:12.488 --> 00:35:14.870
You get a resistance, of
characteristic resistance,

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:16.932
which is called the
Hall resistance.

00:35:16.932 --> 00:35:18.640
And as Edwin Hall has
already figured out

00:35:18.640 --> 00:35:21.350
in the last century, I'll show
this next the next few graph,

00:35:21.350 --> 00:35:24.370
Rxy, this resistance
is proportional to E,

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:26.260
to the magnetic
field, and inversely

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:29.950
proportional to the carrier
density in the material.

00:35:29.950 --> 00:35:32.737
So it's a wonderful way of
measuring carrier densities

00:35:32.737 --> 00:35:34.570
in material, particularly
in semiconductors.

00:35:34.570 --> 00:35:36.712
Because, you see, the
smaller end, the easier

00:35:36.712 --> 00:35:37.420
it is to measure.

00:35:37.420 --> 00:35:39.440
The smaller end, the
bigger this number is.

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:41.200
And typically,
carrier concentrations

00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:43.720
are small in semiconductor
as compared to metals.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:45.400
So this is a wonderful
way of measuring

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:46.420
carrier concentration.

00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:48.860
And it's an industry standard.

00:35:48.860 --> 00:35:51.520
So this is the
first thing you do.

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:55.930
And we've done this, of
course, but Edwin Hall

00:35:55.930 --> 00:35:58.550
had done this already
in the last century.

00:35:58.550 --> 00:36:00.670
This is why it's
called Hall effect.

00:36:00.670 --> 00:36:03.130
He was a graduate
student, by the way,

00:36:03.130 --> 00:36:06.880
at Johns Hopkins in 1879,
when he discovered that

00:36:06.880 --> 00:36:10.004
in a very thin foil of gold.

00:36:10.004 --> 00:36:11.920
He just did this experiment
that I showed you.

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:13.397
And this is the result here.

00:36:13.397 --> 00:36:14.980
In fact, there's
another aspect to all

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:18.130
of this, which I always
enjoy, is for all of us that

00:36:18.130 --> 00:36:20.200
are writing papers that
have to be 3 and 1/2 page

00:36:20.200 --> 00:36:21.351
and not any longer.

00:36:21.351 --> 00:36:23.350
It's interesting to read
this introduction here.

00:36:23.350 --> 00:36:25.480
We have to fight with every
word, take it word out,

00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:27.980
and make an acronym out of it,
so that you save another five

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:28.900
words.

00:36:28.900 --> 00:36:31.610
This starts out with, "Sometimes
during the last university

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:33.130
year, while I was
reading Maxwell's

00:36:33.130 --> 00:36:35.470
Electricity and Magnetism--"
I mean, by that time,

00:36:35.470 --> 00:36:36.340
you had already--

00:36:36.340 --> 00:36:40.150
we're already over your
page limit nowadays.

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:43.630
The other aspect is,
just like it is expensive

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:45.580
nowadays to publish
color figures,

00:36:45.580 --> 00:36:47.650
it must have been
expensive in those days

00:36:47.650 --> 00:36:49.510
to publish any kind of a figure.

00:36:49.510 --> 00:36:52.880
So his results are here
only in form of a table.

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:55.390
So I had to sit down
and, for the first time,

00:36:55.390 --> 00:36:58.110
plot up Edwin Hall's data.

00:36:58.110 --> 00:37:02.140
And here is Edwin Hall's
data now on a graph.

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:03.550
He has plotted.

00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:06.896
This is what we call
nowadays the Hall resistance.

00:37:06.896 --> 00:37:08.770
It's actually a current
through the gold leaf

00:37:08.770 --> 00:37:12.430
divided by the current
through a magnetometer

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:15.750
versus magnetic field in units
of the Earth's magnetism.

00:37:15.750 --> 00:37:17.380
One does not know
what these units are,

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:18.880
but one knows that
it's proportional

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:19.796
to the magnetic field.

00:37:19.796 --> 00:37:20.530
So that's fine.

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:21.490
So this is magnetic field.

00:37:21.490 --> 00:37:22.220
This is Hall resistance.

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:23.553
And you see, those are his data.

00:37:23.553 --> 00:37:27.250
It's a very nice straight
line, just as we know.

00:37:27.250 --> 00:37:29.050
And, of course, since
then, this experiment

00:37:29.050 --> 00:37:31.390
has been done in many
ways by many people,

00:37:31.390 --> 00:37:33.950
and it's a perfectly
nice straight line.

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:37.390
And the slope of this line here
gives you the carrier density

00:37:37.390 --> 00:37:38.440
of the material.

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:41.530
The steeper it is, the
less carriers are in it.

00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:48.240
So this was a status until
1980 when Klaus von Klitzing

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:51.130
came along and told us
that this is not so at all.

00:37:51.130 --> 00:37:54.420
In fact, in two dimensions,
it's not so at all.

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:56.550
This is the original
result, Klitzing, Pepper,

00:37:56.550 --> 00:37:58.194
and Dorda in 1980.

00:37:58.194 --> 00:38:00.360
And it's plotted in a
different way Edwin Hall would

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:01.026
have plotted it.

00:38:01.026 --> 00:38:03.390
So I show you the way--

00:38:03.390 --> 00:38:04.260
similar data.

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:06.330
It's not the same
data, but similar data

00:38:06.330 --> 00:38:10.830
locks if you plot it like
Edwin Hall plotted it.

00:38:10.830 --> 00:38:12.610
Magnetic field Hall resistance.

00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:14.340
This is the Hall resistance.

00:38:14.340 --> 00:38:17.070
I'll talk about the
other one in a second.

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:20.300
So Edwin Hall would have told
us this is a straight line.

00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:22.880
And so Klaus von
Klitzing found--

00:38:22.880 --> 00:38:25.080
of course, this is
a very nice example.

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:27.710
The steps were much
weaker when he saw him.

00:38:27.710 --> 00:38:29.530
But there are lots
of steps in there.

00:38:29.530 --> 00:38:31.090
This is not all a straight line.

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:32.630
These steps are in there.

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:35.930
And not only are these steps
very flat and sometimes very

00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:39.510
wide, but they are also
quantized to units of H

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:42.680
over E squared to an accuracy,
nowadays, of a few parts and 10

00:38:42.680 --> 00:38:43.940
to the 8.

00:38:43.940 --> 00:38:46.010
So a few parts in
a billion, almost,

00:38:46.010 --> 00:38:50.360
a few 10 billionths accurately
quantized to this ratio

00:38:50.360 --> 00:38:52.400
of natural constant.

00:38:52.400 --> 00:38:54.350
This came as a big surprise.

00:38:54.350 --> 00:38:57.980
In fact, there are some inklings
in the theoretical literature

00:38:57.980 --> 00:39:00.300
that sort of foresaw
this in some sense,

00:39:00.300 --> 00:39:02.990
but not really pointed it out.

00:39:02.990 --> 00:39:08.190
You experimentalists
should-- and it was obscure.

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:11.240
But Klaus von Klitzing
discovered this, actually,

00:39:11.240 --> 00:39:15.770
at the Magnet Lab in
Grenoble, the sort of sister

00:39:15.770 --> 00:39:17.940
institution in Europe.

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:19.820
So this is not all
a straight line.

00:39:19.820 --> 00:39:21.850
But it's plateaus
and it's quantized.

00:39:21.850 --> 00:39:23.619
And I show you also
what the resistance

00:39:23.619 --> 00:39:25.160
does as a function
of magnetic field.

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:26.660
That's the other property.

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:28.570
It looks like the
derivative of this.

00:39:28.570 --> 00:39:30.951
So whenever this is flat,
it's actually a deep minimum.

00:39:30.951 --> 00:39:32.450
And then when it
makes a transition,

00:39:32.450 --> 00:39:33.533
you get a spike out of it.

00:39:33.533 --> 00:39:36.380
So the magnetic resistance
looks like the derivative

00:39:36.380 --> 00:39:37.592
of the Hall resistance.

00:39:37.592 --> 00:39:39.050
I show you this
because, later I've

00:39:39.050 --> 00:39:41.050
sometimes show you these
data, because they're

00:39:41.050 --> 00:39:43.610
sort of more dramatic than
even the Hall resistance.

00:39:43.610 --> 00:39:45.890
But it's the same--

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:49.610
the origin is it
has the same origin.

00:39:49.610 --> 00:39:51.800
Here comes my two view
graphs explanation

00:39:51.800 --> 00:39:54.680
of the integer
quantum Hall effect.

00:39:54.680 --> 00:39:58.690
If you put a magnetic field on
a two-dimensional electron gas,

00:39:58.690 --> 00:40:02.930
than the electrons classically
perform rotary motion

00:40:02.930 --> 00:40:04.160
around the magnetic fields.

00:40:04.160 --> 00:40:07.040
So quantum mechanically, this
is just a harmonic oscillator,

00:40:07.040 --> 00:40:09.682
so this is my quantum
mechanical oscillator.

00:40:09.682 --> 00:40:11.390
And then there are
bound states in there.

00:40:11.390 --> 00:40:13.520
And there's only a certain
number of bound-- well,

00:40:13.520 --> 00:40:15.470
there's an infinite
number actually.

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:17.850
But they are discrete, and
there's nothing in between.

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:20.016
So the electron can be in
this state, in this state,

00:40:20.016 --> 00:40:22.756
or in this state, and
nothing in between.

00:40:22.756 --> 00:40:24.380
So in a three-dimensional
electron gas,

00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:26.005
there is actually
something in between.

00:40:26.005 --> 00:40:27.780
But in 2D, there's
nothing in between.

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:29.530
And therefore, there's
only allowed states

00:40:29.530 --> 00:40:32.330
at this energy, at this
energy, and this energy,

00:40:32.330 --> 00:40:33.920
and nothing in between.

00:40:33.920 --> 00:40:37.740
I forget the spin, or leave the
spin out for most of my talk.

00:40:37.740 --> 00:40:39.410
It's just complicates
matters here,

00:40:39.410 --> 00:40:44.290
but it's very easy
to take into account.

00:40:44.290 --> 00:40:47.250
The other aspect of
this quantization

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:49.020
is that the number
of electrons you

00:40:49.020 --> 00:40:53.460
can fit into a squared per
one of these Landau levels,

00:40:53.460 --> 00:40:56.760
as they are called,
is only a ratio

00:40:56.760 --> 00:40:59.340
of a natural constant
in proportion

00:40:59.340 --> 00:41:00.900
to the magnetic field.

00:41:00.900 --> 00:41:03.830
So you take-- if you
take this wave function,

00:41:03.830 --> 00:41:05.270
it makes sort of
a size out of it.

00:41:05.270 --> 00:41:07.020
You take a cookie
cutter and just cut out,

00:41:07.020 --> 00:41:08.430
see how many fit in there.

00:41:08.430 --> 00:41:10.860
And you see that the number
of electrons per level

00:41:10.860 --> 00:41:12.830
is just e times B over h.

00:41:12.830 --> 00:41:14.350
And it's independent
of the mass.

00:41:14.350 --> 00:41:16.570
It's independent of
epsilon of the materials,

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:18.030
independent of everything.

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:21.970
It's eB, only proportional
to the magnetic field.

00:41:21.970 --> 00:41:24.270
So the integral quantum
Hall effect actually

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:29.330
happens when I fill up an
exact number of these states,

00:41:29.330 --> 00:41:32.210
of these Landau levels.

00:41:32.210 --> 00:41:34.760
And you can see
that very easily.

00:41:34.760 --> 00:41:39.420
You take the number of electrons
per level, which is eB over h.

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:43.920
Then if i, 1, 2, 3, 4 of
these levels is filled,

00:41:43.920 --> 00:41:45.790
then it's just i times d.

00:41:45.790 --> 00:41:47.790
You put this into the
equation that I showed you

00:41:47.790 --> 00:41:51.330
earlier that Edwin Hall derived
already in the last century,

00:41:51.330 --> 00:41:53.820
and you get the result,
h divided by ie squared.

00:41:53.820 --> 00:41:56.800
So it's really true.

00:41:56.800 --> 00:41:58.200
There's a big but here.

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:02.310
And this took ingenious
people to figure out.

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:03.840
Because what you
assume in there is

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:08.010
that the number of electrons, in
a certain sense, is quantized,

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:11.010
and that the system is ideal.

00:42:11.010 --> 00:42:12.180
You do it here.

00:42:12.180 --> 00:42:15.240
But it's never like this,
the number of electrons

00:42:15.240 --> 00:42:17.400
is fixed once and for all.

00:42:17.400 --> 00:42:23.700
And you never have
an exact number

00:42:23.700 --> 00:42:27.570
of Landau levels occupied
except for singular points.

00:42:27.570 --> 00:42:29.550
So all this says
is that the long--

00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:33.540
right, this is my Hall line here
along the magnetic field line.

00:42:33.540 --> 00:42:35.160
There's a few discrete points.

00:42:35.160 --> 00:42:37.210
But when I go up,
this resistance

00:42:37.210 --> 00:42:39.556
is exactly this number.

00:42:39.556 --> 00:42:41.430
But it doesn't say that
there's any plateaus.

00:42:41.430 --> 00:42:43.570
I wouldn't even
know where they are.

00:42:43.570 --> 00:42:45.390
So this is a bit of a
fake doing this way.

00:42:45.390 --> 00:42:48.220
But I cannot go
much deeper into it.

00:42:48.220 --> 00:42:49.980
What it tells you is
that this connection

00:42:49.980 --> 00:42:54.524
between the quantization
of the Hall resistance

00:42:54.524 --> 00:42:56.940
and the two-dimensional system,
the two-dimension electron

00:42:56.940 --> 00:42:59.310
system has something
to do with filling up

00:42:59.310 --> 00:43:02.190
exact numbers of Landau levels.

00:43:02.190 --> 00:43:06.570
And this big but here has
to do with localization

00:43:06.570 --> 00:43:07.590
in two dimensions.

00:43:07.590 --> 00:43:09.390
There's actually an
important aspect.

00:43:09.390 --> 00:43:11.790
If you had an ideal
two-dimension system,

00:43:11.790 --> 00:43:13.740
you would not see
the quantum effect.

00:43:13.740 --> 00:43:16.450
It requires dirt
in order to see it.

00:43:16.450 --> 00:43:19.200
I think it's this sort of
totally counterintuitive.

00:43:19.200 --> 00:43:20.190
You need dirt.

00:43:20.190 --> 00:43:21.150
You need impurities.

00:43:21.150 --> 00:43:25.560
You need localization of
electrons in order to see it.

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:27.560
In fact, the guy
that figured it out

00:43:27.560 --> 00:43:30.830
is going to give a
talk sometime today.

00:43:30.830 --> 00:43:33.800
This is the--

00:43:33.800 --> 00:43:36.620
I will not go through
this argument, of course.

00:43:36.620 --> 00:43:41.060
But the bottom line
off of Bob's argument,

00:43:41.060 --> 00:43:44.970
one way at least of looking
at it, is that Rxy, this--

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:46.439
you can actually regard it--

00:43:46.439 --> 00:43:48.480
which is this number--
you can actually regard it

00:43:48.480 --> 00:43:51.830
as a ratio of flux,
of magnetic flux,

00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:53.540
quantum to the
electronic charge.

00:43:53.540 --> 00:43:56.870
So it's the ratio of flux
quantum to electronic charge.

00:43:56.870 --> 00:43:59.930
And therefore, you can look at
this, the quantum Hall effect,

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:01.700
if you turn this
argument around,

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:03.920
as being a measure of e.

00:44:03.920 --> 00:44:06.320
So that is a very
accurate measurement

00:44:06.320 --> 00:44:08.360
of the electronic
charge, which is then

00:44:08.360 --> 00:44:11.439
the flux quantum divided
by whatever you measure.

00:44:11.439 --> 00:44:12.605
And then this is an integer.

00:44:12.605 --> 00:44:15.063
And the integer you always know
whether it's 1, 2, 3, or 4.

00:44:15.063 --> 00:44:16.740
You cannot be that far off.

00:44:16.740 --> 00:44:20.120
So it is one way of looking
at the integer quantum Hall

00:44:20.120 --> 00:44:21.980
effect that is a very
accurate measurement

00:44:21.980 --> 00:44:25.190
of the electronic charge.

00:44:25.190 --> 00:44:29.720
The other aspect that I
always found perplexing

00:44:29.720 --> 00:44:33.350
is that you measure something
to an accuracy of a few parts

00:44:33.350 --> 00:44:36.830
10 to the 8 in a sample that
looks as terrible as that one.

00:44:36.830 --> 00:44:40.370
This is about three millimeters
wide, about 15 millimeter long.

00:44:40.370 --> 00:44:43.470
This is indium here that
is put on and diffused

00:44:43.470 --> 00:44:44.920
in order to make contact.

00:44:44.920 --> 00:44:47.420
The black part is actually the
surface of the semiconductor,

00:44:47.420 --> 00:44:49.340
which is really shiny.

00:44:49.340 --> 00:44:51.350
These parts have
been scratched away

00:44:51.350 --> 00:44:57.650
with an ultrasonic scratcher,
really a very intrusive method.

00:44:57.650 --> 00:45:00.740
And those are gold
wires running off here.

00:45:00.740 --> 00:45:03.640
This is a sample that
was made by Dan Tsui.

00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:05.450
I would have done
a much nicer job,

00:45:05.450 --> 00:45:09.200
but I'm sure that my contacts
wouldn't have worked.

00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:09.990
Dan's worked.

00:45:09.990 --> 00:45:11.656
And, in fact, that's
the sample in which

00:45:11.656 --> 00:45:14.030
we discovered the fractional
quantum Hall effect.

00:45:14.030 --> 00:45:18.470
So in terms of the integral
quantum Hall effect already,

00:45:18.470 --> 00:45:21.890
it is always so amazing
that, independent

00:45:21.890 --> 00:45:25.250
of geometry, independent of
how you put your contacts on,

00:45:25.250 --> 00:45:27.620
you measure something, a
quantity to a few parts 10

00:45:27.620 --> 00:45:28.267
to the 8.

00:45:28.267 --> 00:45:30.350
And, of course, this is
the essence of a standard,

00:45:30.350 --> 00:45:33.200
that it does not depend
on, in this case,

00:45:33.200 --> 00:45:34.970
on the geometry at all.

00:45:34.970 --> 00:45:38.689
You can even make a round
sample with all the contacts

00:45:38.689 --> 00:45:39.230
on the edges.

00:45:39.230 --> 00:45:42.930
As long as you do that,
you also measure it.

00:45:42.930 --> 00:45:45.290
This is a picture
that's probably

00:45:45.290 --> 00:45:48.440
going to bring some emotions
up in some of the people that

00:45:48.440 --> 00:45:50.600
are sitting here in the
audience, certainly, Larry,

00:45:50.600 --> 00:45:52.910
I would think.

00:45:52.910 --> 00:45:54.680
This is-- I don't
know which cell it is,

00:45:54.680 --> 00:45:55.610
but Larry can tell us.

00:45:55.610 --> 00:45:58.260
Which cell is it, Larry?

00:45:58.260 --> 00:46:01.430
It was downstairs
someplace, yeah, I think so.

00:46:01.430 --> 00:46:04.550
This is 23 Tesla, I suppose.

00:46:04.550 --> 00:46:07.461
Picture that was shot much
later, dilution refrigerator--

00:46:07.461 --> 00:46:08.210
that's the fridge.

00:46:08.210 --> 00:46:09.780
This is the can for around it.

00:46:09.780 --> 00:46:10.930
This is the pumping system.

00:46:10.930 --> 00:46:13.660
A person is about as big
as this pumping system.

00:46:13.660 --> 00:46:19.010
And we went with samples
through the Magnet Lab

00:46:19.010 --> 00:46:22.882
and did experiments in
high magnetic fields,

00:46:22.882 --> 00:46:24.840
and later on then with
a dilution refrigerator.

00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:26.800
This is about 10 years
later, I would think,

00:46:26.800 --> 00:46:31.790
but still when the Francis
Bitter National Magnet

00:46:31.790 --> 00:46:35.566
Lab was in--

00:46:35.566 --> 00:46:36.332
up here.

00:46:36.332 --> 00:46:38.540
Well, it still is up here,
only without the National.

00:46:42.950 --> 00:46:46.180
Dan and I were coming up
here for several years

00:46:46.180 --> 00:46:48.100
to do experiments.

00:46:48.100 --> 00:46:51.760
And we were typically getting
up on Sunday morning, driving up

00:46:51.760 --> 00:46:55.360
here, having a long
four-hour chat.

00:46:55.360 --> 00:46:56.860
Depending on who
was driving, it was

00:46:56.860 --> 00:46:59.310
either three or four hours--

00:46:59.310 --> 00:47:01.180
four or five, I
should say, I think.

00:47:01.180 --> 00:47:03.220
We're not that fast.

00:47:03.220 --> 00:47:07.920
And we had this brand new
sample form Art Gossard.

00:47:07.920 --> 00:47:10.750
We were doing experiments
down in whatever cell.

00:47:10.750 --> 00:47:13.240
I don't know which cell it was.

00:47:13.240 --> 00:47:15.760
We were working on
silicon MOSFETs, actually.

00:47:15.760 --> 00:47:18.200
Dan had invited me
to come up with him.

00:47:18.200 --> 00:47:20.830
He was there already since
quite a while doing experiments

00:47:20.830 --> 00:47:21.880
on silicon.

00:47:21.880 --> 00:47:23.530
And then with this
new sample, we

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:27.070
started working together on
this modulation dopes systems.

00:47:27.070 --> 00:47:30.430
So here's the result
of October, 6, 1981.

00:47:30.430 --> 00:47:31.780
This is the Hall resistance.

00:47:31.780 --> 00:47:33.746
This is the magnetic
field, just what

00:47:33.746 --> 00:47:34.870
Edwin Hall would have done.

00:47:34.870 --> 00:47:37.027
And it's a very
nice straight line.

00:47:37.027 --> 00:47:38.860
This is an offset here,
so they get it still

00:47:38.860 --> 00:47:40.550
on the chart recorder paper.

00:47:40.550 --> 00:47:42.130
It's an old technology
to take data.

00:47:42.130 --> 00:47:44.860
You take a pen and pull
it over a piece of paper.

00:47:44.860 --> 00:47:46.210
And then you look at the trace.

00:47:46.210 --> 00:47:49.060
And that's what
your data is like.

00:47:49.060 --> 00:47:51.060
There is-- actually,
this is my handwriting--

00:47:51.060 --> 00:47:52.990
this says laser upstairs.

00:47:52.990 --> 00:47:55.169
You see these spikes here?

00:47:55.169 --> 00:47:56.710
There was-- I don't
know which laser.

00:47:56.710 --> 00:47:58.210
There was a powerful
laser upstairs,

00:47:58.210 --> 00:48:00.830
and whenever they yanked it,
we got a big signal down there.

00:48:00.830 --> 00:48:02.785
So this is from
the laser upstairs.

00:48:02.785 --> 00:48:05.470
We just had to make sure
that this was not physics,

00:48:05.470 --> 00:48:07.210
but just a third effect.

00:48:07.210 --> 00:48:09.450
But otherwise, it's just
a nice straight line,

00:48:09.450 --> 00:48:11.804
just as Edwin Hall
would have said.

00:48:11.804 --> 00:48:13.720
But they're already
deviations coming up here.

00:48:13.720 --> 00:48:15.970
And these deviations are from
the integral quantum Hall

00:48:15.970 --> 00:48:16.270
effect.

00:48:16.270 --> 00:48:18.519
We knew exactly where they
were, where they had to be.

00:48:20.930 --> 00:48:24.040
So the top line is
basically a repetition

00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:26.680
of this, taken at 4 degrees.

00:48:26.680 --> 00:48:29.020
And when we pumped lower, to
lower temperatures, indeed,

00:48:29.020 --> 00:48:31.250
these structures became
nice flat Hall plateaus.

00:48:31.250 --> 00:48:33.120
And that was the
last Hall plateau,

00:48:33.120 --> 00:48:34.600
on the second last Hall plateau.

00:48:34.600 --> 00:48:37.660
Everything, all the
world was in order.

00:48:37.660 --> 00:48:43.020
But it got out of order up here.

00:48:43.020 --> 00:48:46.450
What you see here is that the
magnetic field is about three

00:48:46.450 --> 00:48:47.830
times as high as this one.

00:48:47.830 --> 00:48:49.690
Remember, this is the
last Hall plateau.

00:48:49.690 --> 00:48:52.960
This is the last time
the Fermi energy can

00:48:52.960 --> 00:48:54.280
be between two Landau levels.

00:48:54.280 --> 00:48:56.780
From then on, it's always
in the lowest Landau level.

00:48:56.780 --> 00:48:58.580
So there was nothing
expected out here.

00:48:58.580 --> 00:49:00.400
Actually, I should
say, the reason

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:04.240
we looked at these things was
because Dan Tsui at the time

00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:05.830
wanted to look at
the Wigner lattice.

00:49:05.830 --> 00:49:07.330
He had done that
already in silicon.

00:49:07.330 --> 00:49:09.430
And we thought, well, let's
look at these materials

00:49:09.430 --> 00:49:10.800
and see where the
Wigner lattice.

00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:13.008
The Wigner lattice is this
when the electron condense

00:49:13.008 --> 00:49:15.660
into a regular
array of electrons,

00:49:15.660 --> 00:49:18.189
a hexagonal array, or
triangular lattice-- triangular,

00:49:18.189 --> 00:49:18.730
I should say.

00:49:18.730 --> 00:49:20.530
It's not hexagonal--
triangular lattice

00:49:20.530 --> 00:49:22.120
of electrons that
become immobilized

00:49:22.120 --> 00:49:24.370
and they form a nice
two-dimensional lattice.

00:49:24.370 --> 00:49:26.545
This was a prediction back then.

00:49:26.545 --> 00:49:28.420
There are now some
indications over the years

00:49:28.420 --> 00:49:30.520
that something like
that indeed exists.

00:49:30.520 --> 00:49:32.580
And that's what we
were looking for.

00:49:32.580 --> 00:49:34.550
And what we saw is
this plateau there.

00:49:34.550 --> 00:49:35.830
Well, it's not a plateau yet.

00:49:35.830 --> 00:49:39.040
But it's sort of a
inflection point.

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:41.290
We got all excited about it.

00:49:41.290 --> 00:49:43.600
But, of course, it could
be all sort of things.

00:49:43.600 --> 00:49:46.660
I mean, samples, resistances
very often shot up

00:49:46.660 --> 00:49:47.530
or shot down.

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:51.010
But this one looked
sort of clean up here,

00:49:51.010 --> 00:49:52.460
but then it
developed this thing,

00:49:52.460 --> 00:49:54.418
which just looks like
the integral quantum Hall

00:49:54.418 --> 00:49:58.300
effect at a higher temperature,
but at a different point.

00:49:58.300 --> 00:50:03.400
And we were standing there
down in whatever cell floor.

00:50:03.400 --> 00:50:05.930
And in about 15 seconds,
Dan did the following.

00:50:05.930 --> 00:50:09.280
And I always tell this, because
I think it's just wonderful

00:50:09.280 --> 00:50:12.010
and tells you a
lot about Dan Tsui.

00:50:12.010 --> 00:50:15.170
He took this into his finger,
the distance from here to here.

00:50:15.170 --> 00:50:18.320
And he counted off how
often he could count it off

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:19.820
until it got to
this magnetic field.

00:50:19.820 --> 00:50:22.950
So it was 1, 2, 3.

00:50:22.950 --> 00:50:25.030
And he said 1, 2, 3 quarks.

00:50:28.435 --> 00:50:30.310
And we both had a very
good chuckle about it,

00:50:30.310 --> 00:50:34.300
because it was so humorous that,
of course, quarks, of course,

00:50:34.300 --> 00:50:38.860
are elementary particles
with which a charge of 1/3

00:50:38.860 --> 00:50:40.339
is associated.

00:50:40.339 --> 00:50:42.880
And, of course, we are not doing
elementary particle physics.

00:50:42.880 --> 00:50:45.170
The highest voltage we
have applied in our system

00:50:45.170 --> 00:50:47.260
is about 10 millielectron volts.

00:50:47.260 --> 00:50:49.510
So this is a small m.

00:50:49.510 --> 00:50:52.420
This is not a capital M.
And it's certainly not a G.

00:50:52.420 --> 00:50:55.090
So it was a good joke.

00:50:55.090 --> 00:50:57.940
But, at the same time,
he was dead right.

00:50:57.940 --> 00:51:01.210
Because that it had something
to do with the charge of 1/3

00:51:01.210 --> 00:51:03.640
really turned out to be correct.

00:51:03.640 --> 00:51:10.610
And that just shows you
tremendous insight of Dan.

00:51:10.610 --> 00:51:11.576
So this is then--

00:51:11.576 --> 00:51:12.950
this is the argument
written out.

00:51:12.950 --> 00:51:14.870
If you take Bob
Laughlin's argument

00:51:14.870 --> 00:51:17.350
at the time, which is that
the electronic charge,

00:51:17.350 --> 00:51:18.920
or whatever you measure,
is just the flux quantum

00:51:18.920 --> 00:51:19.610
divided by this.

00:51:19.610 --> 00:51:21.830
And you take it seriously,
that's what you get out.

00:51:21.830 --> 00:51:24.850
Just put the numbers in, and
this is just this 1, 2, 3.

00:51:24.850 --> 00:51:27.980
It's 1/3 of an electronic
charge somehow involved

00:51:27.980 --> 00:51:30.590
if you take it literally.

00:51:30.590 --> 00:51:33.020
So, of course, at the
time, it wasn't clear

00:51:33.020 --> 00:51:34.670
whether it was
really a step or not.

00:51:34.670 --> 00:51:38.410
After all, it was only
a slight inflection.

00:51:38.410 --> 00:51:40.790
It was only an inflection here.

00:51:40.790 --> 00:51:44.040
So the question was whether
it was a step or not.

00:51:44.040 --> 00:51:48.460
And we had to look closely
whether it actually was a step.

00:51:48.460 --> 00:51:51.440
You see this says here,
this is not a step.

00:51:51.440 --> 00:51:56.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:56.150 --> 00:51:59.210
I often show-- I often
show this view graph.

00:51:59.210 --> 00:52:02.780
And I do it nowadays in
different continents.

00:52:02.780 --> 00:52:06.740
This is the only one
that can laugh about it.

00:52:06.740 --> 00:52:09.950
You go to Europe, they just--
there's just dead silence.

00:52:09.950 --> 00:52:13.220
They don't get this joke.

00:52:13.220 --> 00:52:15.349
And you know why not.

00:52:15.349 --> 00:52:16.640
This country is run by lawyers.

00:52:19.430 --> 00:52:24.080
So going to lower temperatures,
what was an inflection point

00:52:24.080 --> 00:52:26.480
really became a
pretty nice plateau.

00:52:26.480 --> 00:52:28.680
And I always say is a
few parts and 10 to the 4

00:52:28.680 --> 00:52:32.120
that this was at 1/3,
and it was quantized

00:52:32.120 --> 00:52:35.060
to about 3 times H over e--

00:52:35.060 --> 00:52:38.210
so H over 1/3 e squared,
I should probably put it.

00:52:38.210 --> 00:52:40.165
And simultaneously,
the resistance,

00:52:40.165 --> 00:52:42.540
the magnetoresistance, actually
developed a deep minimum,

00:52:42.540 --> 00:52:44.420
which wasn't going
yet all the way down.

00:52:44.420 --> 00:52:45.350
But it looked like it.

00:52:45.350 --> 00:52:49.160
As a function of temperature,
it was going down.

00:52:49.160 --> 00:52:51.780
Another picture of the
time a little bit later,

00:52:51.780 --> 00:52:55.860
down that was now around the
hybrid magnet is this one.

00:52:55.860 --> 00:52:59.440
This was taken in February 16,
1984, about three years later.

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:00.890
But it's the only
one that I got.

00:53:00.890 --> 00:53:02.889
And those were people
that were working with us.

00:53:02.889 --> 00:53:05.650
This is Albert Chang.

00:53:05.650 --> 00:53:09.350
This is Peter Berglund,
Greg Boebinger, Dan Tsui,

00:53:09.350 --> 00:53:11.860
and myself here
holding up a sign.

00:53:11.860 --> 00:53:15.200
85 millikelvin, 80 kilogauss.

00:53:15.200 --> 00:53:18.560
Peter Berglund was the guy
that developed this and built

00:53:18.560 --> 00:53:20.954
this dilution refrigerator
that's sitting down here.

00:53:20.954 --> 00:53:23.120
And there was a post-doc
on a grad student, actually

00:53:23.120 --> 00:53:27.650
an MIT grad student, that
Peter Wolff told us about.

00:53:27.650 --> 00:53:29.360
Peter said, I got a
good grad student.

00:53:29.360 --> 00:53:30.020
You want one?

00:53:30.020 --> 00:53:31.650
And we said, sure.

00:53:31.650 --> 00:53:35.070
And Greg just became a
terrific collaborator.

00:53:35.070 --> 00:53:40.700
He's nowadays director of
the Magnet Lab at Los Alamos.

00:53:40.700 --> 00:53:42.420
There's one section
that's in Los Alamos,

00:53:42.420 --> 00:53:44.540
and Greg is running this now.

00:53:44.540 --> 00:53:46.980
So he's still sticking to
magnets, like most of us.

00:53:46.980 --> 00:53:48.800
We fell in love with magnets.

00:53:48.800 --> 00:53:50.540
Cannot get away from them.

00:53:50.540 --> 00:53:54.890
Must have something to do
with the attractive force.

00:53:54.890 --> 00:53:58.715
So it very quickly became
clear that different

00:53:58.715 --> 00:54:00.340
from the integral
quantum Hall effect--

00:54:00.340 --> 00:54:02.150
and you'll have seen
all this, so I'm not

00:54:02.150 --> 00:54:03.210
going to go through it.

00:54:03.210 --> 00:54:06.890
There was the argument that the
fraction quantum Hall effect is

00:54:06.890 --> 00:54:08.330
not a single particle effect.

00:54:08.330 --> 00:54:13.120
It does not come about from
the interaction between--

00:54:13.120 --> 00:54:16.010
sorry-- from the quantization
of individual electrons

00:54:16.010 --> 00:54:18.320
in the presence of an
electric magnetic field

00:54:18.320 --> 00:54:19.580
in a semiconductor.

00:54:19.580 --> 00:54:22.260
But it had to do with
interaction between electrons.

00:54:22.260 --> 00:54:28.710
It must come from this,
because all of the steps that

00:54:28.710 --> 00:54:30.990
come about from individual--

00:54:30.990 --> 00:54:32.520
quantization of
individual electrons

00:54:32.520 --> 00:54:34.800
were all expressed in the
integer quantum Hall effect

00:54:34.800 --> 00:54:35.580
already.

00:54:35.580 --> 00:54:38.440
There shouldn't
be anything else.

00:54:38.440 --> 00:54:40.570
And there were, I think,
the smartest and brightest

00:54:40.570 --> 00:54:45.240
of the theorists who were
trying to figure it out

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:48.010
for quite a while.

00:54:48.010 --> 00:54:48.890
And this here is Bob.

00:54:51.307 --> 00:54:52.390
And he says, "Nothing yet.

00:54:52.390 --> 00:54:53.830
How about you, Newton?"

00:54:53.830 --> 00:54:58.205
With an apple from hell
about to drop onto him.

00:54:58.205 --> 00:55:00.330
And, of course, you're
going to hear more about it.

00:55:00.330 --> 00:55:04.000
But this is what
he came up with.

00:55:04.000 --> 00:55:06.360
This is his wave function.

00:55:06.360 --> 00:55:09.800
And I'm always--

00:55:09.800 --> 00:55:16.730
I always express that I find
this an immense achievement

00:55:16.730 --> 00:55:20.110
that the behavior
of some 10 to the 11

00:55:20.110 --> 00:55:24.650
electrons you can
describe everything

00:55:24.650 --> 00:55:28.390
with an equation that has
just about 15 digits in them,

00:55:28.390 --> 00:55:29.940
15 letters in them.

00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:31.580
This is Bob Laughlin's
wave function.

00:55:31.580 --> 00:55:33.210
He's going to tell
us more about it.

00:55:33.210 --> 00:55:34.350
But this is the solution.

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:35.970
This is, within
quantum mechanics,

00:55:35.970 --> 00:55:38.610
everything we can know about it,
including all the interactions

00:55:38.610 --> 00:55:40.750
between the electrons.

00:55:40.750 --> 00:55:42.800
Bob will modify that, I suppose.

00:55:46.190 --> 00:55:50.360
Let me tell you about
the fraction quantum Hall

00:55:50.360 --> 00:55:52.940
effect and its origin from
a slightly different point

00:55:52.940 --> 00:55:57.000
of view that we have developed
over the-- when I say we,

00:55:57.000 --> 00:55:57.800
the theorists have.

00:55:57.800 --> 00:55:59.765
And I'm just a few--

00:55:59.765 --> 00:56:05.900
a poor interpretation
of the theorists' work.

00:56:05.900 --> 00:56:08.390
One way of looking at the
fraction of quantum Hall effect

00:56:08.390 --> 00:56:11.570
nowadays is from
the point of view

00:56:11.570 --> 00:56:13.190
of the attachment
of magnetic flux

00:56:13.190 --> 00:56:15.410
to the electrons,
which is driven

00:56:15.410 --> 00:56:17.920
by electron-electron
interaction.

00:56:17.920 --> 00:56:19.807
The electrons try to
be at the position

00:56:19.807 --> 00:56:21.140
where there's no other electron.

00:56:21.140 --> 00:56:23.930
They have to do that for
Pauli principle reasons.

00:56:23.930 --> 00:56:26.030
But the more you get the
electrons away from you,

00:56:26.030 --> 00:56:28.640
the better it is for Coulomb
reason because of the Coulomb

00:56:28.640 --> 00:56:31.190
interaction with
the other electrons.

00:56:31.190 --> 00:56:33.740
So since the wave
function have 0s,

00:56:33.740 --> 00:56:35.840
many particles may
function as also 0s.

00:56:35.840 --> 00:56:39.080
They try to attract as many 0s
of the wave function onto them.

00:56:39.080 --> 00:56:42.310
It is advantageous
in terms of energy.

00:56:42.310 --> 00:56:44.090
And one way of
describing that is

00:56:44.090 --> 00:56:46.790
in terms of what's
called [INAUDIBLE] flux.

00:56:46.790 --> 00:56:50.570
It's similar as if you had
attached to the electrons

00:56:50.570 --> 00:56:53.910
a magnetic flux.

00:56:53.910 --> 00:56:55.710
I won't go any
further into this.

00:56:55.710 --> 00:57:00.360
It takes theorists to do it,
and certainly much more time.

00:57:00.360 --> 00:57:01.870
But then this
particle statistics

00:57:01.870 --> 00:57:02.797
is actually changing.

00:57:02.797 --> 00:57:04.880
If you take two electrons,
you change them around.

00:57:04.880 --> 00:57:07.046
And their fermions, the
wave function gets a minus 1

00:57:07.046 --> 00:57:08.560
in front.

00:57:08.560 --> 00:57:09.670
So they're fermions.

00:57:09.670 --> 00:57:13.150
If you take two electrons
with a vortex attached-- well,

00:57:13.150 --> 00:57:15.430
with a [INAUDIBLE]
flux-- let's just

00:57:15.430 --> 00:57:19.130
call it magnetic flux-- attached
to them, you exchange them,

00:57:19.130 --> 00:57:20.370
you get also this minus sign.

00:57:20.370 --> 00:57:22.084
But because there's
a magnetic field

00:57:22.084 --> 00:57:24.250
and you move the electrons
around the magnetic field

00:57:24.250 --> 00:57:26.710
that's stuck to
the other electron,

00:57:26.710 --> 00:57:28.660
it gets an additional
phase change.

00:57:28.660 --> 00:57:32.830
And therefore, the minus 1 is
multiplied by another minus 1,

00:57:32.830 --> 00:57:34.390
so it's a plus 1.

00:57:34.390 --> 00:57:36.790
If you stick 2 on it, it
becomes a minus 1 again.

00:57:36.790 --> 00:57:39.290
If you take 3, it
becomes a plus 1.

00:57:39.290 --> 00:57:41.050
So these behave like fermions.

00:57:41.050 --> 00:57:42.490
These behave like bosons.

00:57:42.490 --> 00:57:43.750
These behave like fermions.

00:57:43.750 --> 00:57:46.580
These behave-- in fact,
whenever m is odd,

00:57:46.580 --> 00:57:47.680
it looks like a boson.

00:57:47.680 --> 00:57:50.080
And whenever m is even,
it looks like a fermion.

00:57:50.080 --> 00:57:52.360
And there's a long
list of people--

00:57:52.360 --> 00:57:53.970
I couldn't list
everybody on here--

00:57:53.970 --> 00:57:56.200
a long list of people
that, over time,

00:57:56.200 --> 00:57:58.720
have developed this picture.

00:57:58.720 --> 00:58:01.810
So there's a way, which
I call here another way--

00:58:01.810 --> 00:58:05.260
because I don't have
the time to show the--

00:58:05.260 --> 00:58:08.050
and Bob is going to
speak later anyway.

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:11.530
So one way of looking at the
fractional quantum Hall effect

00:58:11.530 --> 00:58:14.320
at 1/3 then is to
take electrons.

00:58:14.320 --> 00:58:17.839
Imagine there are 3 flux quanta
attached to them by virtue

00:58:17.839 --> 00:58:19.630
of the electron-electron
interaction, which

00:58:19.630 --> 00:58:21.463
forces them to have 0s
on the wave function.

00:58:21.463 --> 00:58:24.910
But just look at that as being
magnetic flux attached to them.

00:58:24.910 --> 00:58:27.070
Then I look at these
particles as new particles,

00:58:27.070 --> 00:58:30.250
which are no longer
fermions but bosons now.

00:58:30.250 --> 00:58:32.440
And these new particles now
are green little spheres

00:58:32.440 --> 00:58:35.350
rather than red little spheres
with three arrows on them.

00:58:35.350 --> 00:58:38.170
So I have now a system
of green little balls.

00:58:38.170 --> 00:58:42.580
And the red balls
plus flux quanta

00:58:42.580 --> 00:58:46.307
have been transformed
into these green balls.

00:58:46.307 --> 00:58:48.390
And they are now bosons
rather than the electrons.

00:58:48.390 --> 00:58:49.931
In the magnetic
field, actually, they

00:58:49.931 --> 00:58:51.966
become bosons in
no magnetic field,

00:58:51.966 --> 00:58:53.340
because all of
the magnetic field

00:58:53.340 --> 00:58:56.220
has been sucked in
by the electrons.

00:58:56.220 --> 00:58:57.810
They're attached
to the electrons.

00:58:57.810 --> 00:58:59.602
They don't see the
magnetic fields anymore.

00:58:59.602 --> 00:59:01.226
This is sort of a
jump that I'm making.

00:59:01.226 --> 00:59:03.147
You have to really think
more deeply about it,

00:59:03.147 --> 00:59:05.480
particularly since I'm seeing
it's [INAUDIBLE] flux, not

00:59:05.480 --> 00:59:05.980
real flux.

00:59:05.980 --> 00:59:07.750
There are deep
issues down there.

00:59:07.750 --> 00:59:09.980
But just for the
purpose of this talk,

00:59:09.980 --> 00:59:12.480
we take the electron with three
flux quanta attached to them

00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:14.370
and make out of them
what's now called

00:59:14.370 --> 00:59:18.030
composite bosons in the
absence of magnetic field.

00:59:18.030 --> 00:59:20.370
Then you have a
system, a new system,

00:59:20.370 --> 00:59:24.480
of composite bosons where the
magnetic flux has disappeared.

00:59:24.480 --> 00:59:26.520
And this system then
is at 0 magnetic field.

00:59:26.520 --> 00:59:31.640
And bosons condense into the
bose condensate superficially,

00:59:31.640 --> 00:59:32.310
certainly.

00:59:32.310 --> 00:59:33.990
And, indeed, that's
one of the way

00:59:33.990 --> 00:59:36.427
you can look at the 1/3
state, that you do this,

00:59:36.427 --> 00:59:38.760
take the electrons, put flux
quanta, make new particles,

00:59:38.760 --> 00:59:40.599
and bosons in 0
magnetic field, and they

00:59:40.599 --> 00:59:42.390
condense into a bose
condensate, and that's

00:59:42.390 --> 00:59:44.460
what the 1/3 state is.

00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:46.500
So you can look at it
as a bose compensation

00:59:46.500 --> 00:59:47.880
of composite bosons.

00:59:47.880 --> 00:59:51.060
This is, actually,
historically backwards but.

00:59:51.060 --> 00:59:56.460
It's pedagogically a little
bit easier to do it this way.

00:59:56.460 --> 00:59:57.836
Now, that's the 1/3 state.

00:59:57.836 --> 00:59:59.210
How about all
these other states?

00:59:59.210 --> 01:00:00.570
And there's a slew of them.

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:02.160
The 1/3 was just the first.

01:00:02.160 --> 01:00:04.930
And then there are tons of
other states that came about.

01:00:04.930 --> 01:00:08.000
And here, I show you what is
the magnetoresistance, which

01:00:08.000 --> 01:00:10.690
looks like the derivative
because it's more impressive.

01:00:10.690 --> 01:00:12.440
And I apologize,
actually, for the picture

01:00:12.440 --> 01:00:15.110
because it's old by now.

01:00:15.110 --> 01:00:17.910
In the meantime,
there's probably 5,

01:00:17.910 --> 01:00:19.650
6 more oscillations here.

01:00:19.650 --> 01:00:25.460
So there's 1/3, 2/5 3/7,
4/9, 5/11, 6/13, 7--

01:00:25.460 --> 01:00:27.510
whatever-- up to 21.

01:00:27.510 --> 01:00:29.420
So you see all these other--

01:00:29.420 --> 01:00:32.270
the integer quantum Hall effect
are these deep minima here.

01:00:32.270 --> 01:00:33.775
All the rest is fractions.

01:00:33.775 --> 01:00:34.940
It's not just the 1/3.

01:00:34.940 --> 01:00:38.540
It's just a slew of
things, of fractions.

01:00:38.540 --> 01:00:40.790
This is a list of fractions
that have been seen.

01:00:40.790 --> 01:00:42.980
We can argue about
to what degree we

01:00:42.980 --> 01:00:44.270
trust each of these fractions.

01:00:44.270 --> 01:00:46.550
But this is just my
way of writing it down.

01:00:46.550 --> 01:00:47.605
I say this one is OK.

01:00:47.605 --> 01:00:48.980
And, I think,
actually, recently,

01:00:48.980 --> 01:00:51.410
we just have seen something
that looks like 6/23.

01:00:51.410 --> 01:00:52.190
So it's up here.

01:00:52.190 --> 01:00:54.710
See, it's a slew of
fractions, not just one third.

01:00:54.710 --> 01:00:56.662
It was just the first
one to be discovered.

01:00:56.662 --> 01:00:57.870
So what's the origin of this?

01:00:57.870 --> 01:01:01.627
OK, so it makes it a little
bit busy that suddenly all

01:01:01.627 --> 01:01:02.460
of these fractions--

01:01:02.460 --> 01:01:04.570
A little bit like
these chaps here.

01:01:04.570 --> 01:01:07.630
There's a guy reading
the music down here.

01:01:07.630 --> 01:01:10.030
And you say, gee, look at
all these little black dots.

01:01:10.030 --> 01:01:14.310
It's totally confusing
what's happening there.

01:01:14.310 --> 01:01:19.180
The first thing when
I was looking into--

01:01:19.180 --> 01:01:22.300
and it's this state at 1/2.

01:01:22.300 --> 01:01:24.280
See, there's something at 1/2.

01:01:24.280 --> 01:01:26.350
It doesn't look at all
like these other fraction

01:01:26.350 --> 01:01:27.480
that have this nice minima.

01:01:27.480 --> 01:01:29.650
The 1/2 state is a
broad little thing.

01:01:29.650 --> 01:01:31.690
It doesn't do much as a
function of temperature.

01:01:31.690 --> 01:01:34.760
This is a low temperature of 100
millikelvin, 123 millikelvin,

01:01:34.760 --> 01:01:35.650
perhaps.

01:01:35.650 --> 01:01:36.560
It doesn't do much.

01:01:36.560 --> 01:01:37.690
It just sits there.

01:01:37.690 --> 01:01:40.930
And the 1/2 state was for
long a long time a miracle,

01:01:40.930 --> 01:01:43.870
sort of, what this
thing is all about.

01:01:43.870 --> 01:01:47.470
But then when I heard
the description of it--

01:01:47.470 --> 01:01:53.170
and the people that are most
responsible for it-- actually,

01:01:53.170 --> 01:01:56.050
these few here.

01:01:56.050 --> 01:01:58.360
But then, also, Jainendra
Jain, who actually-- again,

01:01:58.360 --> 01:01:59.651
this is historically backwards.

01:01:59.651 --> 01:02:00.870
I'll get to it.

01:02:00.870 --> 01:02:04.260
So in the same vein of what I
told you about electrons flux

01:02:04.260 --> 01:02:07.170
quanta onto it, you can
then look at the 1/2 state

01:02:07.170 --> 01:02:09.090
where there's only
two flux per electron.

01:02:09.090 --> 01:02:10.500
You attach those to it.

01:02:10.500 --> 01:02:13.830
And as I told you earlier, now,
they look like fermions again.

01:02:13.830 --> 01:02:16.350
But they look like fermions
without a magnetic field,

01:02:16.350 --> 01:02:18.720
or the magnetic fluxes
on the electrons.

01:02:18.720 --> 01:02:22.470
And therefore, you get now
pink particles, pink particles

01:02:22.470 --> 01:02:24.720
that behave like fermions,
but in the absence

01:02:24.720 --> 01:02:26.310
of a magnetic field.

01:02:26.310 --> 01:02:28.710
And therefore, the magnetic
field has been eaten up.

01:02:28.710 --> 01:02:31.410
The electrons are moving around
just like in 0 magnetic field.

01:02:31.410 --> 01:02:34.320
But they're fermions.

01:02:34.320 --> 01:02:35.460
Not but they're fermions.

01:02:35.460 --> 01:02:37.290
They remain fermions
in spite of the fact

01:02:37.290 --> 01:02:41.690
that two flux have attached to
them, or just because actually.

01:02:41.690 --> 01:02:45.650
We know what a system that is
fermions in 0 magnetic field

01:02:45.650 --> 01:02:46.150
does.

01:02:46.150 --> 01:02:47.970
It fills up a Fermi surface.

01:02:47.970 --> 01:02:51.330
That is exactly, actually, what
was found and was predicted

01:02:51.330 --> 01:02:54.360
first, but then was
found experimentally

01:02:54.360 --> 01:02:56.540
in various experiments
by Bob Laughlin-- sorry,

01:02:56.540 --> 01:02:58.680
I wanted to say Bob Willard.

01:02:58.680 --> 01:03:01.320
Bob Laughlin has just become an
experimentalist, what he always

01:03:01.320 --> 01:03:03.090
wanted to be in life.

01:03:03.090 --> 01:03:06.480
So I made you one.

01:03:06.480 --> 01:03:12.210
This is just a caricature,
just a cartoon of it.

01:03:12.210 --> 01:03:15.600
So you find it in
k space, a circle

01:03:15.600 --> 01:03:17.400
like that, the Fermi edge.

01:03:17.400 --> 01:03:20.550
And you can measure this.

01:03:20.550 --> 01:03:21.917
Never mind the fact of 2.

01:03:21.917 --> 01:03:24.000
In fact, this fact-- this
difference of fact of 2,

01:03:24.000 --> 01:03:26.400
actually, square root of
two was an important aspect

01:03:26.400 --> 01:03:29.070
of that, yes, this
really looks like it.

01:03:29.070 --> 01:03:33.360
So this was found that
the electrons at 1/2

01:03:33.360 --> 01:03:37.590
form a new Fermi surface,
just like in 0 magnetic field.

01:03:37.590 --> 01:03:39.360
And then you-- OK,
so we have only 1/2.

01:03:39.360 --> 01:03:41.389
So what of all the
other fractions?

01:03:41.389 --> 01:03:43.930
In all the other fractions then,
one can look at in this way.

01:03:43.930 --> 01:03:45.180
And that's really,
historically, sort

01:03:45.180 --> 01:03:46.470
of the starting point of it.

01:03:46.470 --> 01:03:50.450
But for this talk,
better to do this way.

01:03:50.450 --> 01:03:51.830
You remember this view graph.

01:03:51.830 --> 01:03:54.163
That was one of the very first
view graphs I showed you.

01:03:54.163 --> 01:03:56.570
Just integral quantum
Hall effect, quantization.

01:03:56.570 --> 01:04:00.680
This is the 2D surface,
quantized Hall--

01:04:00.680 --> 01:04:02.740
Landau levels, et cetera.

01:04:02.740 --> 01:04:04.670
And then one thinks of
all these other states

01:04:04.670 --> 01:04:08.320
in a way that is analogous to
the fractional quantum Hall

01:04:08.320 --> 01:04:09.200
state--

01:04:09.200 --> 01:04:11.070
sorry, to the integer
quantum Hall state.

01:04:11.070 --> 01:04:13.111
See, the integer quantum
Hall state is down here,

01:04:13.111 --> 01:04:16.430
and you get these oscillations
from the integer Hall effect.

01:04:16.430 --> 01:04:20.390
Now we have another 0 of
magnetic field, so to say,

01:04:20.390 --> 01:04:23.990
at 1/2, where all the magnetic
field has been eaten up.

01:04:23.990 --> 01:04:26.510
And if I slightly change
the magnetic field,

01:04:26.510 --> 01:04:29.960
then I get a residual
magnetic field.

01:04:29.960 --> 01:04:31.600
And these particles
here that see

01:04:31.600 --> 01:04:33.890
0 magnetic field is
actually exactly 1/2,

01:04:33.890 --> 01:04:37.520
see a finite magnetic field
if you get a little bit off.

01:04:37.520 --> 01:04:39.350
And then they develop
Landau levels.

01:04:39.350 --> 01:04:41.980
Just like the electrons develop
Landau levels down here,

01:04:41.980 --> 01:04:44.270
these new particles,
composite fermions,

01:04:44.270 --> 01:04:46.430
develop Landau
levels by themselves.

01:04:46.430 --> 01:04:49.040
And these Landau levels
of the composite fermions

01:04:49.040 --> 01:04:51.710
become the fractional
quantum Hall states.

01:04:51.710 --> 01:04:54.170
So you can look at the
fractional quantum Hall

01:04:54.170 --> 01:04:57.180
effect also as the
integral quantum effects,

01:04:57.180 --> 01:05:01.880
but not of regular electrons,
but of composite fermions.

01:05:01.880 --> 01:05:04.970
And there's a very
nice symmetry.

01:05:04.970 --> 01:05:08.870
This is just the same
plot only, one's in red

01:05:08.870 --> 01:05:10.160
and one's in black.

01:05:10.160 --> 01:05:11.960
And if I pull this
over here, where

01:05:11.960 --> 01:05:14.870
I put the 1/2 on top
of the 0, then you

01:05:14.870 --> 01:05:19.910
see that the black minima, which
are from the fractional quantum

01:05:19.910 --> 01:05:21.520
Hall effect, and the
red minima, which

01:05:21.520 --> 01:05:23.311
were from the integral
quantum Hall effect,

01:05:23.311 --> 01:05:25.280
are just on top of each other.

01:05:25.280 --> 01:05:28.230
So we should have seen
this experimentally.

01:05:28.230 --> 01:05:29.900
But it was really
the theorists that

01:05:29.900 --> 01:05:32.900
were pushing down our throat.

01:05:32.900 --> 01:05:34.700
I suppose I have
about two minutes.

01:05:34.700 --> 01:05:36.550
Is this correct?

01:05:36.550 --> 01:05:38.240
OK.

01:05:38.240 --> 01:05:43.130
So this picture is now
confirmed in many ways.

01:05:43.130 --> 01:05:48.230
There have been experiments done
on these fractional states that

01:05:48.230 --> 01:05:52.700
then let you extrapolate back to
some kind of a mess around 1/2.

01:05:52.700 --> 01:05:54.980
There's a lot of questions
still associated what's

01:05:54.980 --> 01:05:57.740
happening exactly at 1/2.

01:05:57.740 --> 01:05:58.460
What is the mass?

01:05:58.460 --> 01:05:59.570
What does it really mean?

01:06:02.150 --> 01:06:04.320
What kind of phenomena
do you see there exactly?

01:06:04.320 --> 01:06:05.750
What else could you see?

01:06:05.750 --> 01:06:07.622
Is it a real Fermi
system or not?

01:06:07.622 --> 01:06:09.830
These are all things that
are up in the air and still

01:06:09.830 --> 01:06:12.080
experiments being done on it.

01:06:12.080 --> 01:06:16.490
But the concept of a composite
fermion, composite boson

01:06:16.490 --> 01:06:19.960
is really something that
has become very important

01:06:19.960 --> 01:06:22.660
in the fractional
quantum Hall effect.

01:06:22.660 --> 01:06:27.830
The spin of the electron
also has back in

01:06:27.830 --> 01:06:30.470
and became a very
important tool to measure

01:06:30.470 --> 01:06:32.510
some of the energy gaps.

01:06:32.510 --> 01:06:35.090
I don't have time
to get into it.

01:06:35.090 --> 01:06:37.770
We can actually associate a mass
with these composite fermions.

01:06:37.770 --> 01:06:41.420
We can associate a G factor
with the composite fermions.

01:06:41.420 --> 01:06:44.630
All this has been done
over the past few years.

01:06:44.630 --> 01:06:49.910
Let me show you just one other
state that is of great interest

01:06:49.910 --> 01:06:51.200
nowadays.

01:06:51.200 --> 01:06:54.200
And that's a state
out here, at 5/2.

01:06:54.200 --> 01:06:55.544
Never mind how I get to this.

01:06:55.544 --> 01:06:56.210
This is the 1/2.

01:06:56.210 --> 01:06:57.020
This is the 3/2.

01:06:57.020 --> 01:06:58.422
This guy is called 5/2.

01:06:58.422 --> 01:07:00.380
It basically means, for
every five flux quanta,

01:07:00.380 --> 01:07:01.999
you get 2 electrons.

01:07:01.999 --> 01:07:03.790
And this state looks
sort of like this one.

01:07:03.790 --> 01:07:06.248
But when you cool-- this state
looks sort of like this one.

01:07:06.248 --> 01:07:08.855
But when you cool
it down, it doesn't.

01:07:08.855 --> 01:07:10.980
And this is the newest
experiments on that.

01:07:10.980 --> 01:07:14.370
When you cool the 5/2 state
down-- sorry, it's a bit busy--

01:07:14.370 --> 01:07:16.500
it goes to 0, and
it gets a plateau.

01:07:16.500 --> 01:07:20.370
Now, this is a 1/2 state, so
it has an even denominator.

01:07:20.370 --> 01:07:22.920
So it should be a fermion,
and fermions do not

01:07:22.920 --> 01:07:24.750
show a fractional
quantum Hall effect.

01:07:24.750 --> 01:07:27.480
Composite fermions do not show a
fractional quantum Hall effect,

01:07:27.480 --> 01:07:29.260
but they show a Fermi surface.

01:07:29.260 --> 01:07:31.260
This one shows a fractional
quantum Hall effect.

01:07:31.260 --> 01:07:35.950
It was discovered in '87, I
think, Bob Willard, et al.

01:07:35.950 --> 01:07:39.074
And that was a big surprise,
an even denominator

01:07:39.074 --> 01:07:40.740
became a fractional
quantum Hall effect.

01:07:40.740 --> 01:07:42.917
How come?

01:07:42.917 --> 01:07:43.750
We still don't know.

01:07:43.750 --> 01:07:45.083
Well, let me actually point out.

01:07:45.083 --> 01:07:47.350
This is the lowest temperature
ever have cooled down

01:07:47.350 --> 01:07:48.700
in an electron system.

01:07:48.700 --> 01:07:51.890
These data were just taken
at the Magnet Lab in--

01:07:51.890 --> 01:07:53.320
Talla-- sorry, not Tallahassee--

01:07:53.320 --> 01:07:56.710
Gainesville-- where they have
very low temperatures, milli--

01:07:56.710 --> 01:07:58.264
microkelvin, for that matter.

01:07:58.264 --> 01:07:59.680
We use it only
down to millikelvin

01:07:59.680 --> 01:08:01.107
in high magnetic fields.

01:08:01.107 --> 01:08:02.440
This is not that high, actually.

01:08:02.440 --> 01:08:04.900
This is only 3.7 Tesla.

01:08:04.900 --> 01:08:07.810
But we were able to cool
the electron system down

01:08:07.810 --> 01:08:09.772
to 4 millikelvin, which
is a real achievement--

01:08:09.772 --> 01:08:11.980
the elections, not the
lattice-- this is the electron

01:08:11.980 --> 01:08:15.400
temperature-- by various
trick that the people

01:08:15.400 --> 01:08:18.229
at the Gainesville,
[INAUDIBLE] and then together

01:08:18.229 --> 01:08:21.189
with our student
down there, Wei Pan,

01:08:21.189 --> 01:08:24.109
developed that really
let us do this.

01:08:24.109 --> 01:08:28.356
Now, the speculation-- and
there still is a speculation,

01:08:28.356 --> 01:08:30.189
although the theorists
tell us more and more

01:08:30.189 --> 01:08:31.540
that they're very
convinced about this.

01:08:31.540 --> 01:08:33.581
And they even tell us that
our data is supported.

01:08:33.581 --> 01:08:35.439
I don't understand
that argument yet.

01:08:35.439 --> 01:08:39.819
Is that what's happening
is just like at 1/2,

01:08:39.819 --> 01:08:42.609
the electrons actually
do form fermions.

01:08:42.609 --> 01:08:45.250
They take an electron,
put 2 flux quanta onto it,

01:08:45.250 --> 01:08:46.689
and they are fermions.

01:08:46.689 --> 01:08:49.240
So now we have b equal
to 0 with new fermions

01:08:49.240 --> 01:08:51.220
called composite fermions.

01:08:51.220 --> 01:08:54.460
But then they discover that they
can actually mutually attract

01:08:54.460 --> 01:08:55.960
pairwise.

01:08:55.960 --> 01:08:57.870
This is an interaction
in real space.

01:08:57.870 --> 01:09:00.100
But, in fact, the
wave function that you

01:09:00.100 --> 01:09:02.439
can write down for it--
it's called a pfaffian.

01:09:02.439 --> 01:09:05.109
I'm way out of my league here,
but it's called a pfaffian.

01:09:05.109 --> 01:09:10.000
But it looks similar to BCS wave
function with an l equal to one

01:09:10.000 --> 01:09:10.720
in there.

01:09:10.720 --> 01:09:14.529
But there is an interaction
in there, and they form pairs.

01:09:14.529 --> 01:09:18.850
And these pairs-- sort of in
far analogy with Cooper pairs,

01:09:18.850 --> 01:09:20.050
if you think of it--

01:09:20.050 --> 01:09:21.010
they can condense.

01:09:21.010 --> 01:09:23.200
They form a new
quantum liquid now.

01:09:23.200 --> 01:09:25.680
So you form first fermions.

01:09:25.680 --> 01:09:27.430
Then you have an
interaction which is just

01:09:27.430 --> 01:09:29.109
all Coulomb interaction.

01:09:29.109 --> 01:09:30.839
And they form pairs,
and these pairs then

01:09:30.839 --> 01:09:32.380
open up a new gap,
which is something

01:09:32.380 --> 01:09:34.460
like a superconducting gap.

01:09:34.460 --> 01:09:37.210
So in a sense, they form a new
kind of superconductor made out

01:09:37.210 --> 01:09:39.010
of composite
fermions, and that's

01:09:39.010 --> 01:09:42.310
what you see in the experiment.

01:09:42.310 --> 01:09:44.020
Let me leave it at that.

01:09:44.020 --> 01:09:48.069
There are various other
experiments that are very--

01:09:48.069 --> 01:09:50.819
other parts of the
magnetic field scale.

01:09:50.819 --> 01:09:54.440
There are, nowadays,
anisotropies

01:09:54.440 --> 01:09:57.560
that are being discovered
in high magnetic fields.

01:09:57.560 --> 01:09:59.870
They're called stripe phases.

01:09:59.870 --> 01:10:01.880
There is the
question, for example,

01:10:01.880 --> 01:10:04.120
how many electrons
are required in order

01:10:04.120 --> 01:10:05.980
to see the fractional
quantum Hall effect?

01:10:05.980 --> 01:10:07.460
Obviously, one is
not good enough.

01:10:07.460 --> 01:10:10.240
I say obviously, but this
is an important part.

01:10:10.240 --> 01:10:11.300
One is not good enough.

01:10:11.300 --> 01:10:12.760
It's a many particle effect.

01:10:12.760 --> 01:10:14.260
And some people
like Ray and others

01:10:14.260 --> 01:10:16.600
are looking whether 3 is
enough or 5 is enough,

01:10:16.600 --> 01:10:19.307
or do you need 10
in order to see it.

01:10:19.307 --> 01:10:20.890
There's a Wigner
solid out there where

01:10:20.890 --> 01:10:23.470
electrons are
supposed to condense

01:10:23.470 --> 01:10:25.930
into a solid of electrons.

01:10:25.930 --> 01:10:28.710
And that one, there
are indications for it.

01:10:28.710 --> 01:10:30.160
There are things
called skyrmions,

01:10:30.160 --> 01:10:33.659
which are many particle
spin systems that

01:10:33.659 --> 01:10:34.450
are playing a role.

01:10:34.450 --> 01:10:39.380
So this field is alive and
well, to say the least.

01:10:39.380 --> 01:10:42.034
I believe the 5/2 state, if
we really can pin it down,

01:10:42.034 --> 01:10:43.450
that at this kind
of a state, it's

01:10:43.450 --> 01:10:47.200
really opening up a whole
new universe of experiments

01:10:47.200 --> 01:10:48.790
that can be done.

01:10:48.790 --> 01:10:52.230
Let me show you my
last view graph,

01:10:52.230 --> 01:10:56.080
and that is sort of
where are we going.

01:10:56.080 --> 01:10:59.860
This is the data, or the
result that Edwin Hall gave us

01:10:59.860 --> 01:11:01.500
about 100 years ago.

01:11:01.500 --> 01:11:05.260
Magnetic field, Hall
effect, straight line.

01:11:05.260 --> 01:11:07.840
So this is just the
standard Hall effect.

01:11:07.840 --> 01:11:09.880
Then Klaus von Klitzing
came along and told us

01:11:09.880 --> 01:11:11.402
that this is not so.

01:11:11.402 --> 01:11:13.610
There's actually-- you see
this is a used view graph.

01:11:13.610 --> 01:11:15.477
I better fix it up.

01:11:15.477 --> 01:11:16.810
There's these big steps in here.

01:11:16.810 --> 01:11:19.360
This is the integral
quantum Hall effect.

01:11:19.360 --> 01:11:24.310
And then Dan and I were so
lucky to discover the 1/3 state.

01:11:24.310 --> 01:11:26.900
So suddenly, there
were more steps here.

01:11:26.900 --> 01:11:31.040
And then, the 5 something
came along, and the 7, and 11,

01:11:31.040 --> 01:11:34.250
and the 13, and
the 17, and the 23.

01:11:34.250 --> 01:11:35.750
And as we keep
going, we're going

01:11:35.750 --> 01:11:38.984
to end up with that, which
is a perfectly straight line.

01:11:38.984 --> 01:11:41.150
Only what you don't see is
that there are infinitely

01:11:41.150 --> 01:11:43.100
many steps along here.

01:11:43.100 --> 01:11:44.930
It's called the
Devil's staircase,

01:11:44.930 --> 01:11:46.940
Devil for obvious reasons.

01:11:46.940 --> 01:11:49.790
And it's going to be
fractions all over the place.

01:11:49.790 --> 01:11:53.420
So eventually, we're going to
end up just like Edwin Hall,

01:11:53.420 --> 01:11:56.690
and he's going to tell
us, I told you so.

01:11:56.690 --> 01:12:01.040
So let me just show you
this graph here, which

01:12:01.040 --> 01:12:03.170
is probably the most important.

01:12:03.170 --> 01:12:04.040
All of this--

01:12:04.040 --> 01:12:07.100
I only change pump oil.

01:12:07.100 --> 01:12:09.940
Of course, Dan,
who's going to talk,

01:12:09.940 --> 01:12:11.800
long friend and
collaborator and, really,

01:12:11.800 --> 01:12:13.870
my mentor in many ways.

01:12:13.870 --> 01:12:16.840
Lots of collaborators,
post-docs,

01:12:16.840 --> 01:12:19.090
students at Bell
Labs that have worked

01:12:19.090 --> 01:12:22.630
on the fractional
quantum Hall effect.

01:12:22.630 --> 01:12:26.650
And then there is
particularly the people

01:12:26.650 --> 01:12:31.240
that are the crystal growers
that are here pointed out in--

01:12:31.240 --> 01:12:34.570
all important ones here in red,
Art Gossard and Willy Wiegmann,

01:12:34.570 --> 01:12:37.480
and then also Loren
Pfeiffer and Ken West

01:12:37.480 --> 01:12:39.550
who are growing the
samples nowadays.

01:12:39.550 --> 01:12:42.490
So it was these people working
on the fractional quantum Hall

01:12:42.490 --> 01:12:44.560
effect that really
brought it all about.

01:12:44.560 --> 01:12:46.960
I always feel that
Dan and I, we were

01:12:46.960 --> 01:12:50.530
so lucky to discover-- it's
a little bit like archeology.

01:12:50.530 --> 01:12:52.480
We discovered the
first tile sticking out

01:12:52.480 --> 01:12:54.880
from a roof out of
the sand, and then it

01:12:54.880 --> 01:12:59.590
was all these terrific people
that continued the work

01:12:59.590 --> 01:13:01.769
and really were
cleaning up the whole--

01:13:01.769 --> 01:13:03.310
not cleaning up,
actually discovering

01:13:03.310 --> 01:13:09.500
the whole building, which
finally made us to receive

01:13:09.500 --> 01:13:10.580
the Nobel Prize for it.

01:13:10.580 --> 01:13:13.120
I feel like the luckiest
person in the world

01:13:13.120 --> 01:13:15.560
having been able to work
with such terrific people

01:13:15.560 --> 01:13:18.540
and being part of this endeavor.

01:13:18.540 --> 01:13:22.100
So, in a sense, I think I owe
a lot of-- not in a sense,

01:13:22.100 --> 01:13:25.130
I do owe a lot of gratitude
to all of these people that

01:13:25.130 --> 01:13:26.180
have brought it about.

01:13:26.180 --> 01:13:30.020
I feel that I was only
one of the lucky ones that

01:13:30.020 --> 01:13:32.770
was just there at the right
time at the right place.

01:13:32.770 --> 01:13:34.395
Thanks very much.

01:13:34.395 --> 01:13:43.529
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:43.529 --> 01:13:45.320
And, of course, the
right place at the time

01:13:45.320 --> 01:13:49.972
was sort of one
street down the road.

01:13:49.972 --> 01:13:51.680
ASHOORI: Thanks for
a beautiful overview.

01:13:51.680 --> 01:13:53.348
You don't get away so soon.

01:13:53.348 --> 01:13:54.782
Are there questions?

01:13:57.342 --> 01:13:59.050
STORMER: I don't want
to cut into Dan's--

01:13:59.050 --> 01:13:59.799
ASHOORI: Marc?

01:13:59.799 --> 01:14:03.955
KASTNER: First,
couldn't it have been

01:14:03.955 --> 01:14:07.140
done without the Magnet Lab?

01:14:07.140 --> 01:14:07.884
STORMER: No.

01:14:07.884 --> 01:14:09.540
KASTNER: [INAUDIBLE]

01:14:09.540 --> 01:14:10.710
STORMER: Yes, of course.

01:14:10.710 --> 01:14:11.280
No, no.

01:14:11.280 --> 01:14:11.780
Look, no doubt.

01:14:11.780 --> 01:14:13.488
We see the fractional
quantum Hall effect

01:14:13.488 --> 01:14:17.340
now at 0.8 kilogauss,
something like this.

01:14:17.340 --> 01:14:18.669
So this is exaggerated.

01:14:18.669 --> 01:14:19.710
But it's a few kilogauss.

01:14:19.710 --> 01:14:21.190
So, yes, of course today.

01:14:21.190 --> 01:14:23.010
But at this time, no.

01:14:23.010 --> 01:14:24.640
This sample is required.

01:14:24.640 --> 01:14:27.180
This sample, which
was 90,000 mobility,

01:14:27.180 --> 01:14:28.892
in the field of whatever
it was, 19 Tesla

01:14:28.892 --> 01:14:30.600
or something like that
were this is where

01:14:30.600 --> 01:14:32.650
the inflection was to see it.

01:14:32.650 --> 01:14:34.770
At any lower than that,
it wouldn't have happened.

01:14:34.770 --> 01:14:36.870
Nowadays, we have
superconducting magnets

01:14:36.870 --> 01:14:37.710
that go that high.

01:14:37.710 --> 01:14:41.410
At that point superconducting
magnets were typically 8 Tesla.

01:14:41.410 --> 01:14:42.660
So one would not have seen it.

01:14:42.660 --> 01:14:44.820
Yes, at that point,
it required the sample

01:14:44.820 --> 01:14:47.640
and this magnetic field.

01:14:47.640 --> 01:14:49.650
A few years later,
if actually it

01:14:49.650 --> 01:14:51.930
had been pursued any
further, maybe people

01:14:51.930 --> 01:14:54.300
would have said, well,
forget modulation doping.

01:14:54.300 --> 01:14:56.200
It doesn't look interesting.

01:14:56.200 --> 01:14:59.080
But a few years later,
you could do it at home,

01:14:59.080 --> 01:15:03.077
meaning in a superconducting
magnet, but not at the time.

01:15:03.077 --> 01:15:03.910
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:15:03.910 --> 01:15:04.910
ASHOORI: - Bob--

01:15:04.910 --> 01:15:07.910
AUDIENCE: Of course,
there are two prototypes

01:15:07.910 --> 01:15:10.347
I know about for the 1/2.

01:15:13.221 --> 01:15:18.011
And they're distinguished,
so you allegedly

01:15:18.011 --> 01:15:19.450
can find the edge properties.

01:15:19.450 --> 01:15:20.400
STORMER: I what?

01:15:20.400 --> 01:15:21.180
AUDIENCE: Edge--

01:15:21.180 --> 01:15:22.014
STORMER: Edge state?

01:15:22.014 --> 01:15:23.888
AUDIENCE: Are you guys
currently working on--

01:15:23.888 --> 01:15:24.920
STORMER: No.

01:15:24.920 --> 01:15:28.140
This is-- edge states are
always the next generation.

01:15:28.140 --> 01:15:30.140
I mean, you first
have to get the bulk.

01:15:30.140 --> 01:15:31.140
Let me not call it bulk.

01:15:31.140 --> 01:15:34.230
You first have to get
whatever we are doing,

01:15:34.230 --> 01:15:39.000
the trivial stuff, right before
you look into the edge states.

01:15:39.000 --> 01:15:41.480
Tunneling into the edge
state is a difficult problem.

01:15:41.480 --> 01:15:43.063
You know, I mean,
it's still-- there's

01:15:43.063 --> 01:15:45.820
lots of question about
what you really see when

01:15:45.820 --> 01:15:47.890
you chiral systems, et cetera.

01:15:47.890 --> 01:15:51.432
So I don't have anything.

01:15:51.432 --> 01:15:53.140
Maybe Dan is going to
tell you something.

01:15:53.140 --> 01:15:57.960
But I think-- no, we don't
have anything on the edge.

01:15:57.960 --> 01:16:00.160
You know, the other
thing to differentiate

01:16:00.160 --> 01:16:03.712
the two states is whether it's
been polarized or unpolarized.

01:16:03.712 --> 01:16:05.170
And that's already
a difficult one.

01:16:05.170 --> 01:16:07.503
That's the experiment that
we wanted to do since a year.

01:16:07.503 --> 01:16:09.610
But it's so hard to turn
a sample at 4 millikelvin

01:16:09.610 --> 01:16:12.100
electron temperature down
in a high magnetic field

01:16:12.100 --> 01:16:16.180
and see whether one sees the
lifting of the spin degeneracy.

01:16:16.180 --> 01:16:18.610
That, I think, is a
trivial experiment.

01:16:18.610 --> 01:16:19.720
Although, it's very hard.

01:16:19.720 --> 01:16:21.760
And then comes the edge.

01:16:21.760 --> 01:16:24.970
ASHOORI: Why do they call the
5/2 state a superconductor,

01:16:24.970 --> 01:16:27.441
when the longitudinal
conductance doesn't-- it goes

01:16:27.441 --> 01:16:27.940
to 0?

01:16:27.940 --> 01:16:30.880
So it's that--

01:16:30.880 --> 01:16:31.930
STORMER: You know why.

01:16:31.930 --> 01:16:40.050
I mean, it's-- the analogy with
superconductivity is a long

01:16:40.050 --> 01:16:40.550
one.

01:16:40.550 --> 01:16:42.300
There's lots of things
that are different.

01:16:42.300 --> 01:16:45.240
But since it looks like-- it
has sort of the character of BCS

01:16:45.240 --> 01:16:47.160
wave function in there,
so you can look at it

01:16:47.160 --> 01:16:48.840
as being superconductivity.

01:16:48.840 --> 01:16:52.410
But then, of course,
everything else is different.

01:16:52.410 --> 01:16:55.135
Don't catch me at
my weak points.

01:16:55.135 --> 01:16:55.760
ASHOORI: Peter?

01:16:55.760 --> 01:16:56.426
AUDIENCE: Horst?

01:16:56.426 --> 01:16:57.215
STORMER: Yes?

01:16:57.215 --> 01:17:02.307
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
Is there anybody

01:17:02.307 --> 01:17:04.490
else you can think of
who's sitting here?

01:17:04.490 --> 01:17:06.420
STORMER: I don't think so.

01:17:06.420 --> 01:17:07.430
I don't think so.

01:17:10.740 --> 01:17:13.320
I mean, there is a
description of these states

01:17:13.320 --> 01:17:15.946
here, a theoretical description.

01:17:15.946 --> 01:17:17.820
I think all the connections
are not yet made.

01:17:17.820 --> 01:17:19.470
In fact, it was
theoretically predicted

01:17:19.470 --> 01:17:20.730
that there was stripe phases.

01:17:20.730 --> 01:17:24.810
Let's also keep in mind there's
a prediction of stripe phases--

01:17:24.810 --> 01:17:26.950
something.

01:17:26.950 --> 01:17:29.790
And then there are experiments
that are shown anisotropy.

01:17:29.790 --> 01:17:33.390
So far, the connection
that the anisotropy

01:17:33.390 --> 01:17:36.910
is due to stripe phases
had not really been made.

01:17:36.910 --> 01:17:40.024
We just think this is the area
where there is anisotropy.

01:17:40.024 --> 01:17:42.190
This is the area where there
should be stripe phase.

01:17:42.190 --> 01:17:43.450
Probably, that's what it is.

01:17:43.450 --> 01:17:45.040
One doesn't really know that.

01:17:45.040 --> 01:17:47.710
What has been discovered
is anisotropy.

01:17:47.710 --> 01:17:50.980
But it's probably stripe phases.

01:17:50.980 --> 01:17:54.555
From point of theory, I think
we got the experts in here.

01:17:54.555 --> 01:17:55.055
What?

01:17:55.055 --> 01:17:56.450
AUDIENCE: Almost certainly yes.

01:17:56.450 --> 01:17:56.950
STORMER: OK.

01:17:56.950 --> 01:17:57.921
This is one--

01:17:57.921 --> 01:17:58.420
Patrick?

01:17:58.420 --> 01:18:00.292
[LAUGHTER]

01:18:00.292 --> 01:18:05.420
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:18:05.420 --> 01:18:07.670
ASHOORI: So we sit back at
this time in the Magnet Lab

01:18:07.670 --> 01:18:09.407
when you came up
with Horst first law

01:18:09.407 --> 01:18:10.990
that I teach my
students all the time.

01:18:10.990 --> 01:18:11.930
STORMER: Horst first law?

01:18:11.930 --> 01:18:13.080
I haven't heard about this.

01:18:13.080 --> 01:18:16.100
ASHOORI: That crummiest looking
samples always work best.

01:18:16.100 --> 01:18:16.600
STORMER: Oh.

01:18:16.600 --> 01:18:18.970
Yeah, I guess I learned
this from Dan in many ways.

01:18:24.260 --> 01:18:24.760
ASHOORI: OK.

01:18:24.760 --> 01:18:27.330
If there are no more questions,
let's thank Horst again.

01:18:27.330 --> 01:18:31.438
[APPLAUSE]

01:18:31.438 --> 01:18:37.070
LEE: Well, it is a real pleasure
to welcome Dan Tsui to MIT.

01:18:37.070 --> 01:18:44.480
Dan, soon after he got
his PhD from Chicago then

01:18:44.480 --> 01:18:47.900
went to Bell Labs, where he
spent about 15 years before he

01:18:47.900 --> 01:18:50.150
went to Princeton
University as a professor

01:18:50.150 --> 01:18:52.850
in the electrical
engineering department.

01:18:52.850 --> 01:18:58.460
I got to know Dan in the '70s
when we were both in Bell Labs.

01:18:58.460 --> 01:19:04.120
And, as some of you might
know, especially in those days,

01:19:04.120 --> 01:19:07.850
Bell Labs was known for having a
bunch of very aggressive people

01:19:07.850 --> 01:19:09.020
around.

01:19:09.020 --> 01:19:11.870
Well, there are a few
exceptions to this rule,

01:19:11.870 --> 01:19:14.940
and Dan is one of them.

01:19:14.940 --> 01:19:18.320
But Dan may not be the
one who walks the fastest

01:19:18.320 --> 01:19:22.670
or talks the fastest, but when
it comes to matters that count,

01:19:22.670 --> 01:19:26.050
it doesn't take long for one
to figure out that he's usually

01:19:26.050 --> 01:19:28.440
the one that gets there first.

01:19:28.440 --> 01:19:31.070
I had the experience,
for example,

01:19:31.070 --> 01:19:34.670
in the late '70s
when a lot of us

01:19:34.670 --> 01:19:37.910
were doing work on the
localization, weak localization

01:19:37.910 --> 01:19:40.180
effects, and disorder
effects, and so on.

01:19:40.180 --> 01:19:42.140
Dan was really very quick.

01:19:42.140 --> 01:19:46.414
He instantly understood the
importance of what's going on,

01:19:46.414 --> 01:19:47.330
understood the theory.

01:19:47.330 --> 01:19:49.610
And he realized immediately
that the MOSFET system

01:19:49.610 --> 01:19:54.650
is the ideal system to
study these new theories,

01:19:54.650 --> 01:19:57.350
because you can control
the electron density

01:19:57.350 --> 01:19:58.670
and everything else.

01:19:58.670 --> 01:20:02.900
So within a matter of a month
or two, he and David Bishop

01:20:02.900 --> 01:20:06.980
came up with the experiment,
a definitive experiment that

01:20:06.980 --> 01:20:09.080
showed all these weak
localization effects,

01:20:09.080 --> 01:20:10.370
and so on.

01:20:10.370 --> 01:20:12.680
And, of course, the
next great achievement

01:20:12.680 --> 01:20:18.140
was the fractional quantum Hall
effect that we'll hear about.

01:20:18.140 --> 01:20:23.300
Since that time, I
came to MIT in '82.

01:20:23.300 --> 01:20:24.860
And one of the
great pleasures is

01:20:24.860 --> 01:20:29.450
to have periodic visits from Dan
when he comes to do experiments

01:20:29.450 --> 01:20:30.440
at the Magnet Lab.

01:20:30.440 --> 01:20:32.240
And every time he
would come and he

01:20:32.240 --> 01:20:35.300
would show me all
these new data that he

01:20:35.300 --> 01:20:39.230
found that he's excited about
that he wants to understand.

01:20:39.230 --> 01:20:42.140
But every time I
talk to Dan, I always

01:20:42.140 --> 01:20:43.880
come away with the
impression that he's

01:20:43.880 --> 01:20:48.410
someone who is really fascinated
by nature, by what he's doing.

01:20:48.410 --> 01:20:54.210
He loves being at the frontier
and exploring this new world.

01:20:54.210 --> 01:20:56.390
And he is someone who
really wants to understand

01:20:56.390 --> 01:20:58.250
what he's studying.

01:20:58.250 --> 01:21:02.810
And to him, getting
credit, or being the first,

01:21:02.810 --> 01:21:05.510
or getting honor
seems to be secondary.

01:21:05.510 --> 01:21:08.310
These are things that would
come naturally for him.

01:21:08.310 --> 01:21:11.000
Science is really
what drives them.

01:21:11.000 --> 01:21:14.300
And so it's wonderful
that this great honor

01:21:14.300 --> 01:21:17.090
has come to someone who
really does not seek it.

01:21:17.090 --> 01:21:20.357
And so I present to you Dan.

01:21:20.357 --> 01:22:14.579
[APPLAUSE]

01:22:14.579 --> 01:22:15.620
TSUI: Thank you, Patrick.

01:22:19.270 --> 01:22:25.000
It is an honor and
always a pleasure

01:22:25.000 --> 01:22:27.790
to be back on MIT campus.

01:22:31.540 --> 01:22:35.530
I think Horst
probably would say--

01:22:35.530 --> 01:22:38.540
well, I enjoyed
it so much, I even

01:22:38.540 --> 01:22:44.790
spent my first sabbatical
in the Magnet Lab,

01:22:44.790 --> 01:22:49.376
in the Francis Bitter
Magnet Lab, which--

01:23:08.034 --> 01:23:15.880
which I wanted to,
for the talk, is

01:23:15.880 --> 01:23:24.540
to take this opportunity to
have a closer look of some

01:23:24.540 --> 01:23:29.050
of the longstanding
problems as characterized

01:23:29.050 --> 01:23:33.510
by some of the questions--

01:23:38.770 --> 01:23:42.040
some of the questions
and the comments

01:23:42.040 --> 01:23:46.720
we used to hear very often.

01:23:46.720 --> 01:23:50.020
So more specifically,
what I'm going

01:23:50.020 --> 01:23:53.380
to do is I'm going
to give you a very

01:23:53.380 --> 01:23:58.760
brief superficial overview,
basically relation

01:23:58.760 --> 01:24:03.900
the overall, the other
even-denominator fractions,

01:24:03.900 --> 01:24:05.920
which Horst already
touched upon.

01:24:09.860 --> 01:24:16.110
But before I go into any
detail, into those experiments,

01:24:16.110 --> 01:24:21.580
I'm going to spend some
time to, how you say,

01:24:21.580 --> 01:24:27.580
tunneling experiment
into the sample edges.

01:24:27.580 --> 01:24:29.290
And I don't think I'll--

01:24:29.290 --> 01:24:32.320
or Richard and I
thought I'll also

01:24:32.320 --> 01:24:38.790
go through some of the
microwave absorption experiments

01:24:38.790 --> 01:24:43.140
in the very small
fractional limit, where

01:24:43.140 --> 01:24:46.650
the system, the
electrons are insulating,

01:24:46.650 --> 01:24:48.900
are true insulators.

01:24:48.900 --> 01:24:53.070
And if you like, it's a belief--

01:24:53.070 --> 01:24:56.670
well, it is a punitive
linear crystal regime.

01:24:56.670 --> 01:25:01.110
But I probably will not
have time to go into that.

01:25:01.110 --> 01:25:07.450
So this is what I
would like to do.

01:25:07.450 --> 01:25:10.870
Let me-- I have this
view graph here just

01:25:10.870 --> 01:25:22.910
to remind me to say that in our
modern day electronics world,

01:25:22.910 --> 01:25:29.830
and mostly, device
functions are performed

01:25:29.830 --> 01:25:33.100
by these two-dimensional
electrons, which

01:25:33.100 --> 01:25:38.410
confine to move along
the interface of two

01:25:38.410 --> 01:25:45.190
different semiconductors, or a
semiconductor and an insulator.

01:25:45.190 --> 01:25:47.820
But the only point
I want to make here

01:25:47.820 --> 01:25:56.500
is under normal
ambience, these electrons

01:25:56.500 --> 01:26:03.160
behaves as just ordinary GaAs
of two-dimensional particles.

01:26:06.499 --> 01:26:10.600
The strange, or the
extraordinary physics,

01:26:10.600 --> 01:26:22.090
which Horst has shown us becomes
dominant, or very apparent,

01:26:22.090 --> 01:26:28.320
when you take a system at
low temperatures exposed

01:26:28.320 --> 01:26:34.380
to high magnetic field, and when
the system actually is clean.

01:26:34.380 --> 01:26:36.930
It's a clean
two-dimensional system.

01:26:36.930 --> 01:26:38.550
That's the point.

01:26:38.550 --> 01:26:42.240
But the other point
I want to make

01:26:42.240 --> 01:26:47.010
is, really, the only reason
I want to show that view

01:26:47.010 --> 01:26:48.060
graph before--

01:26:48.060 --> 01:26:49.980
actually, let me put it back.

01:26:49.980 --> 01:26:59.880
Because in the magnetic
field, the energy,

01:26:59.880 --> 01:27:02.390
or the so-called
Landau quantization

01:27:02.390 --> 01:27:08.640
quantizes the energy spectrum
into discrete levels.

01:27:08.640 --> 01:27:12.330
If you like, they're massively
degenerate discrete levels.

01:27:12.330 --> 01:27:16.500
And these levels are
called Landau levels.

01:27:16.500 --> 01:27:19.270
And the point I
want to make here

01:27:19.270 --> 01:27:22.690
is mainly the terminology
I want to keep straight.

01:27:22.690 --> 01:27:27.180
So the Landau level
index, this big N,

01:27:27.180 --> 01:27:31.920
is usually integers 0,
1, 2, 3, large numbers,

01:27:31.920 --> 01:27:33.990
you know, whatever numbers.

01:27:33.990 --> 01:27:37.980
So when you take the
electron spin into account,

01:27:37.980 --> 01:27:43.800
each Landau level composed--
or has two spin levels.

01:27:43.800 --> 01:27:46.740
That's the only
point I want to make,

01:27:46.740 --> 01:27:50.060
because what we need to
keep in mind to look at some

01:27:50.060 --> 01:27:53.850
of the data in some detail.

01:27:53.850 --> 01:27:54.350
OK.

01:27:56.900 --> 01:28:03.720
We have already heard
from Horst's talk

01:28:03.720 --> 01:28:08.430
that in the so-called fractional
quantum Hall effect regime,

01:28:08.430 --> 01:28:11.420
correlation is everything.

01:28:11.420 --> 01:28:19.630
And the-- for the odd
denominator fractions--

01:28:19.630 --> 01:28:26.890
which we know the systems go
into so-called Hall liquids.

01:28:26.890 --> 01:28:29.040
At the odd-denominator
quantum Hall liquids,

01:28:29.040 --> 01:28:33.590
we have these fractional
states, you have seen.

01:28:33.590 --> 01:28:41.150
But also, we have seen there is
the even-denominator fractions.

01:28:41.150 --> 01:28:45.320
Correlation favors normal
metal-like Fermi sea

01:28:45.320 --> 01:28:49.820
of composite particles,
or the composite Fermions.

01:28:49.820 --> 01:28:58.660
And this is the case for
the lowest Landau level,

01:28:58.660 --> 01:29:03.260
if you like, the n
equal to 0 Landau level.

01:29:03.260 --> 01:29:10.510
And this is the data, actually
from very recent sample,

01:29:10.510 --> 01:29:16.200
Loren Pfeiffer and Ken
West grow out in September.

01:29:16.200 --> 01:29:24.490
And the data was taken by Wei
Pan, who is graduate student

01:29:24.490 --> 01:29:27.470
Horst and I supervised.

01:29:27.470 --> 01:29:33.570
And what you notice here,
the filling factor 2

01:29:33.570 --> 01:29:36.050
is somewhere here, and down.

01:29:36.050 --> 01:29:42.110
So this is for the n
equal to 0 Landau level.

01:29:42.110 --> 01:29:45.010
So there will be two spin--

01:29:45.010 --> 01:29:48.840
if a spin split, it would
spin up down here somewhere,

01:29:48.840 --> 01:29:51.650
and have the spin down here.

01:29:51.650 --> 01:29:53.620
OK.

01:29:53.620 --> 01:29:58.060
So in this level, indeed,
the composite fermion

01:29:58.060 --> 01:30:02.250
is what the ground state for
even denominator fractions

01:30:02.250 --> 01:30:04.330
is indicated here.

01:30:04.330 --> 01:30:08.570
And, for example, the 1/2,
you have already heard from--

01:30:08.570 --> 01:30:12.370
Horst already showed to
you, except in this sample,

01:30:12.370 --> 01:30:15.100
because the perfection
of the 2D electron

01:30:15.100 --> 01:30:18.370
system, this [INAUDIBLE],,
I think Horst said

01:30:18.370 --> 01:30:25.990
is, denominator
21, or 20 whatever.

01:30:25.990 --> 01:30:30.070
And here is similarly for 1/4.

01:30:30.070 --> 01:30:35.600
And the main thing is for
the even fraction where

01:30:35.600 --> 01:30:39.900
the composite fermion
is the ground state,

01:30:39.900 --> 01:30:44.540
the structure is
essentially featureless

01:30:44.540 --> 01:30:48.850
and is flanked by two series.

01:30:48.850 --> 01:30:52.000
In this case, it's
the p over for p

01:30:52.000 --> 01:30:56.170
plus minus series of
fractional quantum Hall states.

01:30:56.170 --> 01:31:01.180
And here is a p over
2p plus minus 1 series

01:31:01.180 --> 01:31:03.210
of fractional Hall states.

01:31:03.210 --> 01:31:07.820
And similarly for
the 3/4 and 3/2.

01:31:07.820 --> 01:31:13.210
So for the n equal to
0 Landau level, indeed,

01:31:13.210 --> 01:31:17.590
the odd-denominator fractions
are the fractional quantum Hall

01:31:17.590 --> 01:31:18.910
liquids.

01:31:18.910 --> 01:31:25.112
The even-denominator fractions
are these composite fermion

01:31:25.112 --> 01:31:25.611
states.

01:31:30.760 --> 01:31:34.390
For the higher Landau level--
that's what I want to show

01:31:34.390 --> 01:31:35.470
here--

01:31:35.470 --> 01:31:38.630
and that, apparently,
is not the case.

01:31:38.630 --> 01:31:41.980
The data looks very different.

01:31:41.980 --> 01:31:47.230
Again, here is the lowest
Landau level n equal to 0.

01:31:47.230 --> 01:31:49.360
You see that the
even-denominator fraction

01:31:49.360 --> 01:31:51.660
is essentially featureless.

01:31:51.660 --> 01:31:57.720
Just the fractional quantum
Hall states converges unto this.

01:31:57.720 --> 01:31:59.270
The fractional
quantum Hall state

01:31:59.270 --> 01:32:04.060
is this deep minimum and just
convergence into this even

01:32:04.060 --> 01:32:08.410
composite fermion, the ground
state, the composite fermion

01:32:08.410 --> 01:32:11.790
gas state, essentially
featureless.

01:32:11.790 --> 01:32:13.870
But when you go
to the next Landau

01:32:13.870 --> 01:32:16.140
level, and you go to one level--

01:32:16.140 --> 01:32:20.690
that's for the 7/2 and 5/2--

01:32:20.690 --> 01:32:23.360
and you have deep
minimum, which is

01:32:23.360 --> 01:32:27.710
very similar to the
fractional quantum states.

01:32:27.710 --> 01:32:34.670
And also, these structures,
you know, on the flanks,

01:32:34.670 --> 01:32:39.270
on the sides, those are indeed
the fractional quantum Hall

01:32:39.270 --> 01:32:40.650
states.

01:32:40.650 --> 01:32:41.570
OK.

01:32:41.570 --> 01:32:47.560
Now, when you go to the next
higher level, the index is--

01:32:47.560 --> 01:32:48.710
and you could do 2.

01:32:51.881 --> 01:32:58.290
And you actually see are
large spikes at the 9/2, 11/2.

01:32:58.290 --> 01:33:03.920
And similar for the next level,
and you could do 3, 13/2, 15/2.

01:33:03.920 --> 01:33:07.250
These actually have large peaks.

01:33:07.250 --> 01:33:11.210
But more importantly,
whether they are a peak

01:33:11.210 --> 01:33:14.480
or a minimum, a
very deep minimum,

01:33:14.480 --> 01:33:17.867
depends on how you
do the measurement,

01:33:17.867 --> 01:33:18.950
depend on the measurement.

01:33:18.950 --> 01:33:22.400
The current is measured
in what direction?

01:33:22.400 --> 01:33:25.840
The black's measured along
this direction, defined

01:33:25.840 --> 01:33:31.470
as x direction, and the
other one is the y direction.

01:33:31.470 --> 01:33:33.700
So this is, basically, what--

01:33:33.700 --> 01:33:37.110
well, I'm going to do
is I'll come back--

01:33:37.110 --> 01:33:40.600
I'll come back to
show you more data,

01:33:40.600 --> 01:33:43.290
look at these
higher Landau level.

01:33:43.290 --> 01:33:45.320
And you go to 1,
and you go to 2.

01:33:45.320 --> 01:33:53.100
And pick-- in particular,
just pick out the 5/2 and 9/2

01:33:53.100 --> 01:33:57.280
to go through the
experiment in some detail.

01:33:57.280 --> 01:34:02.710
But before I do that,
I want to address,

01:34:02.710 --> 01:34:05.440
basically, the
other comments you

01:34:05.440 --> 01:34:08.483
have seen about edge states.

01:34:35.520 --> 01:34:40.920
Halperin and Wen-- so we got
one probably sitting somewhere

01:34:40.920 --> 01:34:42.450
in the audience, I know--

01:34:42.450 --> 01:34:49.850
were the first told us how to
think about the sample edges,

01:34:49.850 --> 01:34:53.480
how to think about
the edge states.

01:34:53.480 --> 01:34:57.870
In case of the integer
quantum Hall effect

01:34:57.870 --> 01:35:02.660
is quite easy to visualize
or to picture them.

01:35:02.660 --> 01:35:09.780
And that's what is illustrated
on this view graph.

01:35:09.780 --> 01:35:14.110
If this is the bulk material,
in a strong magnetic field,

01:35:14.110 --> 01:35:17.140
we look at the 1
electron energy levels,

01:35:17.140 --> 01:35:20.680
they are just
these Landau levels

01:35:20.680 --> 01:35:23.580
separated by an
energy gap, which

01:35:23.580 --> 01:35:26.890
is just the cyclotomic energy.

01:35:26.890 --> 01:35:31.860
Now, when I go to the surface,
approaching the surface, sample

01:35:31.860 --> 01:35:35.970
surface, then when the electron
orbit in the magnet field,

01:35:35.970 --> 01:35:42.210
the electron feels the surface
potential, or the surface feel.

01:35:42.210 --> 01:35:44.070
Then it changes.

01:35:44.070 --> 01:35:47.100
It increases the
electron energy.

01:35:47.100 --> 01:35:49.780
So why would my
Landau level would

01:35:49.780 --> 01:35:53.420
have to be drawn in this way?

01:35:53.420 --> 01:35:58.090
So now if I am at the
integer quantum Hall states--

01:35:58.090 --> 01:36:03.250
so my Fermi level is somewhere
between these two levels,

01:36:03.250 --> 01:36:04.770
below are all filled.

01:36:04.770 --> 01:36:08.590
Top, my n, below
n is all filled.

01:36:08.590 --> 01:36:11.340
My n plus 1 is all empty.

01:36:11.340 --> 01:36:14.560
Then, of course, inside
the sample in the bulk

01:36:14.560 --> 01:36:17.490
is an insulator gapped.

01:36:17.490 --> 01:36:19.860
But when come to
the surface, there

01:36:19.860 --> 01:36:23.280
is a state right
at the Fermi level.

01:36:23.280 --> 01:36:25.560
And these states,
or if you like,

01:36:25.560 --> 01:36:28.890
the electrons in
these states are

01:36:28.890 --> 01:36:34.350
one one-dimensional
electron system.

01:36:34.350 --> 01:36:39.285
And this becomes more clear
in the next view graph.

01:36:41.820 --> 01:36:46.680
In the real space, this
is how it would look.

01:36:46.680 --> 01:36:50.890
This, of course, you
would recognize this.

01:36:50.890 --> 01:36:52.890
This is probably some
graduate student's thesis.

01:36:52.890 --> 01:36:55.440
Who else would do
this neat think?

01:36:55.440 --> 01:36:58.050
This is taken from
Matt Grayson's thesis.

01:36:58.050 --> 01:37:02.560
As a matter of fact, the data
going to show are all from him.

01:37:07.200 --> 01:37:10.430
So in the bulk in
quantum Hall effect,

01:37:10.430 --> 01:37:15.940
the levels in all these side
for orbits are all occupied.

01:37:15.940 --> 01:37:18.420
And the next higher energy
level ones are empty.

01:37:18.420 --> 01:37:20.330
It's not shown here.

01:37:20.330 --> 01:37:23.210
But come to the
edges, the edge states

01:37:23.210 --> 01:37:27.200
are just the so-called, in
metaphysics in the '60s,

01:37:27.200 --> 01:37:29.290
we called them the
skipping orbits.

01:37:29.290 --> 01:37:31.430
These are the skipping orbits.

01:37:31.430 --> 01:37:33.460
And because of the
magnetic field,

01:37:33.460 --> 01:37:36.960
they all skip in one direction.

01:37:36.960 --> 01:37:41.100
And the language, the
proper language to describe,

01:37:41.100 --> 01:37:44.260
a fancier language,
they're a motion of chiral.

01:37:44.260 --> 01:37:47.820
They're moving in one direction.

01:37:47.820 --> 01:37:51.830
And this is for
the integer case.

01:37:51.830 --> 01:37:57.150
And there has been a lot of
experiment evidence to this.

01:37:57.150 --> 01:37:59.280
For the fractional case, it's--

01:38:04.080 --> 01:38:07.070
for the fractional case, of
course, again, the bulk--

01:38:07.070 --> 01:38:09.280
in the bulk, it will be gapped.

01:38:09.280 --> 01:38:12.930
The charge of excitation
will have a gap.

01:38:12.930 --> 01:38:17.640
And the edges-- the
electrons and edges

01:38:17.640 --> 01:38:21.270
will count to, again, a
one-dimensional electronic

01:38:21.270 --> 01:38:22.500
system.

01:38:22.500 --> 01:38:25.380
But it's an interacting system.

01:38:25.380 --> 01:38:31.290
And it's well known that for
one-dimensional interacting

01:38:31.290 --> 01:38:38.610
electron system, the
ground state is a liquid.

01:38:38.610 --> 01:38:41.700
And usually it's called a
Luttinger liquid, again,

01:38:41.700 --> 01:38:45.820
because the magnetic
field is called

01:38:45.820 --> 01:38:48.210
the Chiral Luttinger liquid.

01:38:48.210 --> 01:38:53.430
And the main characteristic
of a Luttinger liquid

01:38:53.430 --> 01:38:58.710
is it has phonon like
excitation spectrum.

01:38:58.710 --> 01:39:03.730
So the density states, the
other Fermi lab, if you like,

01:39:03.730 --> 01:39:04.230
vanishes.

01:39:07.320 --> 01:39:11.200
But edge, given
the finite energy

01:39:11.200 --> 01:39:14.580
excitation, the
density of states,

01:39:14.580 --> 01:39:20.190
because it's over some power law
dependence, as indicated here.

01:39:20.190 --> 01:39:27.080
And this would have direct
consequences on the current

01:39:27.080 --> 01:39:31.400
due to tunneling
either into the edge

01:39:31.400 --> 01:39:35.250
or tunneling between the edges.

01:39:35.250 --> 01:39:38.410
So if you look at the
current, with a tunneling

01:39:38.410 --> 01:39:43.220
current into the
edge, fractional edge,

01:39:43.220 --> 01:39:47.960
or the tunneling current
between the fractional edges,

01:39:47.960 --> 01:39:53.042
this will have direct
influence, direct consequences.

01:39:53.042 --> 01:39:54.410
OK.

01:39:54.410 --> 01:40:00.080
The earlier
experiment on this is

01:40:00.080 --> 01:40:04.140
the Milliken, Umbach,
Webb experiment

01:40:04.140 --> 01:40:05.880
where they look
at the temperature

01:40:05.880 --> 01:40:13.360
dependence of the tunneling
current between the edges using

01:40:13.360 --> 01:40:16.350
a point contact geometry.

01:40:16.350 --> 01:40:21.900
But what I'll do, basically
showing the data Matt Grayson

01:40:21.900 --> 01:40:27.970
took, Matt Grayson has, is
following the tunneling,

01:40:27.970 --> 01:40:30.020
which is tunneling
from, essentially,

01:40:30.020 --> 01:40:34.050
a normal metal into the
edge, which in some sense--

01:40:34.050 --> 01:40:35.420
many of you--

01:40:35.420 --> 01:40:38.740
I'll show a view where it
will become very clear.

01:40:38.740 --> 01:40:44.170
If you just remember the
normal metal superconductor

01:40:44.170 --> 01:40:47.630
geometry in superconductivity.

01:40:58.770 --> 01:41:04.250
This is just repeat
what I said already.

01:41:04.250 --> 01:41:09.030
So what I'm going to show is
the experiment Matt Grayson--

01:41:09.030 --> 01:41:14.300
Matt Grayson's data, and using
the sample Loren Pfeiffer

01:41:14.300 --> 01:41:17.080
and Ken West grown.

01:41:17.080 --> 01:41:21.805
This is the edge
cleaved overgrowth Ray

01:41:21.805 --> 01:41:25.970
Ashoori mentioned earlier.

01:41:25.970 --> 01:41:30.430
The structure at the end is you
have the 2 dimensional electron

01:41:30.430 --> 01:41:33.890
system on this side.

01:41:33.890 --> 01:41:39.320
And the 2D electron gas
is confined in a quantum

01:41:39.320 --> 01:41:42.030
well indicated here.

01:41:42.030 --> 01:41:46.410
Then from the edge
cleave overgrowth

01:41:46.410 --> 01:41:50.720
a tunnel barrier is grown,
which is in this case,

01:41:50.720 --> 01:41:53.680
be the aluminum gallium
arsenide tunnel barrier.

01:41:53.680 --> 01:41:57.820
Then very heavily
doped semiconductors

01:41:57.820 --> 01:42:01.720
electron and plus get it out,
which is just a normal metal

01:42:01.720 --> 01:42:02.930
electron.

01:42:02.930 --> 01:42:08.640
So the experiment is to
inject our tunnel electrons

01:42:08.640 --> 01:42:12.650
into the fractional
quantum Hall system.

01:42:12.650 --> 01:42:14.610
That's the experiment.

01:42:14.610 --> 01:42:20.630
And if you remember from the
normal metal superconductor

01:42:20.630 --> 01:42:28.520
tunnel junction, the classical
[INAUDIBLE] tunneling geometry,

01:42:28.520 --> 01:42:30.870
which your measurements
are in a tunnel

01:42:30.870 --> 01:42:36.320
inject electron into
this interacting system,

01:42:36.320 --> 01:42:40.930
the one-dimensional
Luttinger liquid system.

01:42:40.930 --> 01:42:45.540
And if you look at the so-called
tunnel conductance, derivative

01:42:45.540 --> 01:42:51.980
of the curve, you measure
directly the tunneling

01:42:51.980 --> 01:42:54.020
conductance.

01:42:54.020 --> 01:43:01.540
So the experiment is just to
measure the IV characteristics,

01:43:01.540 --> 01:43:03.380
the IV curve.

01:43:03.380 --> 01:43:07.840
And this is just another
view what you have.

01:43:07.840 --> 01:43:11.450
This is for the 1/3
stage, the bulk.

01:43:11.450 --> 01:43:14.740
Charging stations gapped.

01:43:14.740 --> 01:43:18.590
And the edge, or the
actions on the edge, that's

01:43:18.590 --> 01:43:21.490
the one-dimensional
Luttinger liquid.

01:43:21.490 --> 01:43:23.890
And the experiment is
basically just measuring

01:43:23.890 --> 01:43:25.970
the IV characteristic.

01:43:25.970 --> 01:43:30.050
You expect a power
law dependence.

01:43:30.050 --> 01:43:35.780
And the exponent alpha
is basically, well,

01:43:35.780 --> 01:43:37.610
if you see the right--

01:43:37.610 --> 01:43:43.766
that's basically characterized
the edge Luttinger liquid.

01:44:10.830 --> 01:44:14.490
Well, I think I have
another picture.

01:44:18.500 --> 01:44:23.718
This is a more realistic
picture from Matt Grayson.

01:44:33.500 --> 01:44:35.870
This is a 2D electron layer.

01:44:35.870 --> 01:44:39.560
So you can put contacts
so you can characterize

01:44:39.560 --> 01:44:42.290
the fractional quantum
Hall characteristics used

01:44:42.290 --> 01:44:44.270
to characterize your sample.

01:44:44.270 --> 01:44:47.660
And here is the tunneling
the normal electrode

01:44:47.660 --> 01:44:48.950
injecting into that.

01:44:48.950 --> 01:44:51.710
OK.

01:44:51.710 --> 01:44:55.040
So you can get look at the--

01:45:02.810 --> 01:45:07.220
red curve is the
IV characteristic,

01:45:07.220 --> 01:45:09.260
the Hall resistance
characteristic

01:45:09.260 --> 01:45:11.490
of the 2D electron.

01:45:11.490 --> 01:45:13.790
And this is the 1/3 plateau.

01:45:13.790 --> 01:45:15.440
This the 2/5.

01:45:15.440 --> 01:45:16.760
This is the 2/3.

01:45:16.760 --> 01:45:18.300
This is the plateau 1.

01:45:18.300 --> 01:45:21.740
And this is the tunneling IV.

01:45:21.740 --> 01:45:32.370
And if you look at the edge,
add the fractional 1/3 state,

01:45:32.370 --> 01:45:36.840
you would expect the
Chiral Luttinger liquid--

01:45:36.840 --> 01:45:39.870
expect to have a Chiral
Luttinger liquid.

01:45:39.870 --> 01:45:41.610
And the IV, you should have--

01:45:41.610 --> 01:45:48.060
you expect the power law
dependence with an exponent.

01:45:52.980 --> 01:45:55.440
It's expected
actually to be for 3.

01:46:01.840 --> 01:46:08.880
This is the first piece of
data from Albert Chang's

01:46:08.880 --> 01:46:11.070
early experiment.

01:46:11.070 --> 01:46:16.110
And this is right at
filling factor 1/3.

01:46:16.110 --> 01:46:19.190
We know the bulk is gapped.

01:46:19.190 --> 01:46:23.610
And the edge, we expect to
have the Luttinger liquid.

01:46:23.610 --> 01:46:28.210
And there is more
than two decades

01:46:28.210 --> 01:46:32.920
as the power law, clear power
law dependence indicator.

01:46:32.920 --> 01:46:37.180
And the power is
2.7 is sufficiently

01:46:37.180 --> 01:46:40.900
close to the
expected value of 3.

01:46:56.260 --> 01:47:03.130
Which Matt Grayson as he looked
at a large number of samples.

01:47:03.130 --> 01:47:10.730
On each sample, not
only at the 1/3 or 2/3,

01:47:10.730 --> 01:47:14.650
also away from
those at a large--

01:47:14.650 --> 01:47:17.310
you know, just continued
a various magnetic field,

01:47:17.310 --> 01:47:19.540
very close grid look at it.

01:47:19.540 --> 01:47:21.490
How do they look?

01:47:21.490 --> 01:47:24.970
And surprisingly, when
even you're not at 1/3,

01:47:24.970 --> 01:47:26.620
